    'Mary runs a Christian music-lesson studio. John organized a private youth choir. Mary made a rush order of choir robes for John''s choir after John told her, in writing, that the choir had $9,000 in its music fund and no unpaid vendor balances. Mary relied on those statements and extended 60-day credit. John never paid.

In federal court, Mary''s first count alleged fraudulent inducement: John knowingly made false statements about the choir''s finances, and Mary relied on them to her detriment in extending credit. The second count alleged that, although John''s statements were not false and Mary did not rely on them when extending credit, John is in default on the robe account and liable for the unpaid balance. Payment was due before the complaint was filed.', 'Mary runs a Christian music-lesson studio. John organized a private youth choir. Mary made a rush order of choir robes for John''s choir after John told her, in writing, that the choir had $9,000 in its music fund and no unpaid vendor balances. Mary relied on those statements and extended 60-day credit. John never paid.

