    '14208_women_of_the_word', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Verdicts and Judgments', 'Issue and Claim Preclusion',
    'Esther, a well-known Bible teacher and conference speaker, brought a class-action lawsuit in federal court against Kingdom Media Corporation on behalf of all speakers, worship leaders, and breakout session teachers who had participated in the "Women of the Word" national Christian women''s conference series. The class was not predominated by common questions of law or fact—each participant had signed individual contracts, led different sessions on different topics, and alleged slightly different harms to her reputation or future booking opportunities. However, the class sought injunctive relief requiring Kingdom Media to issue corrected promotional materials, update its website and archives, and adopt uniform contract, credit, and attribution policies that would apply to the entire group of participants. At trial, the judge found in favor of Kingdom Media Corporation. Lydia, who had led a single breakout session at one regional event two years earlier, has now brought suit against Kingdom Media asserting the same legal claim and seeking the same injunctive relief that was litigated in the class action. Lydia had no knowledge of the previous class action lawsuit, even though Esther''s attorney mailed notice to the address on file for all participants. Is the court likely to grant Kingdom Media Corporation''s motion to dismiss on the ground that Lydia is bound by the prior class judgment?', 'Esther, a well-known Bible teacher and conference speaker, brought a class-action lawsuit in federal court against Kingdom Media Corporation on behalf of all speakers, worship leaders, and breakout session teachers who had participated in the "Women of the Word" national Christian women''s conference series. The class was not predominated by common questions of law or fact—each participant had signed individual contracts, led different sessions on different topics, and alleged slightly different harms to her reputation or future booking opportunities. However, the class sought injunctive relief requiring Kingdom Media to issue corrected promotional materials, update its website and archives, and adopt uniform contract, credit, and attribution policies that would apply to the entire group of participants. At trial, the judge found in favor of Kingdom Media Corporation. Lydia, who had led a single breakout session at one regional event two years earlier, has now brought suit against Kingdom Media asserting the same legal claim and seeking the same injunctive relief that was litigated in the class action. Lydia had no knowledge of the previous class action lawsuit, even though Esther''s attorney mailed notice to the address on file for all participants. Is the court likely to grant Kingdom Media Corporation''s motion to dismiss on the ground that Lydia is bound by the prior class judgment?', 'Is the court likely to grant Kingdom Media Corporation''s motion to dismiss on the ground that Lydia is bound by the prior class judgment?',
