    'Peter, a traveling Christian music tutor, brought a federal diversity action against Martha, a private piano-studio owner, for $4,800 in unpaid tutoring fees. Martha answered and counterclaimed for $6,500, alleging that Peter breached a written piano-repair agreement and that the promised repair warranty failed. Four weeks after Martha served the counterclaim, Peter had sent no reply to it. Martha moved for entry of default and for a default judgment on the counterclaim. After the clerk entered default, Martha asked the clerk to enter judgment for the exact $6,500. May the clerk enter a default judgment?', 'Peter, a traveling Christian music tutor, brought a federal diversity action against Martha, a private piano-studio owner, for $4,800 in unpaid tutoring fees. Martha answered and counterclaimed for $6,500, alleging that Peter breached a written piano-repair agreement and that the promised repair warranty failed. Four weeks after Martha served the counterclaim, Peter had sent no reply to it. Martha moved for entry of default and for a default judgment on the counterclaim. After the clerk entered default, Martha asked the clerk to enter judgment for the exact $6,500. May the clerk enter a default judgment?', 'May the clerk enter a default judgment?',
