    '18608_barnabas_ladder', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Pretrial Procedures', 'Discovery',
    'Deacon Barnabas, a longtime member and volunteer coordinator at Grace Fellowship Church, was injured when a ladder slipped during a Saturday service project to build a wheelchair ramp for an elderly widow in the congregation. He sues the volunteer crew supervisor for negligence. In response to the defendant''s interrogatory asking for the identity of every physician or healthcare provider who examined or treated him for the claimed injuries, Barnabas lists only Dr. Silas, his regular physician, and Dr. Titus, the orthopedist who performed follow-up care.

One month later, while preparing a settlement package, Barnabas discovers a chart note from a free Christian medical clinic he visited the day after the fall (his daughter''s idea because regular offices were closed for the weekend). A visiting retired missionary physician, Dr. Paul, examined the leg, noted significant swelling and tenderness, and wrote that the mechanism was consistent with a fall from height and that X-rays should be considered. The defendant has received no other information about Dr. Paul or this visit.

A: Wait until trial and disclose the third physician only if the defendant objects

B: Nothing, because discovery responses are judged only when served

C: Timely supplement or correct the response unless the information has otherwise been made known to the defendant

D: Supplement only if the defendant serves a second interrogatory asking for updates', 'Deacon Barnabas, a longtime member and volunteer coordinator at Grace Fellowship Church, was injured when a ladder slipped during a Saturday service project to build a wheelchair ramp for an elderly widow in the congregation. He sues the volunteer crew supervisor for negligence. In response to the defendant''s interrogatory asking for the identity of every physician or healthcare provider who examined or treated him for the claimed injuries, Barnabas lists only Dr. Silas, his regular physician, and Dr. Titus, the orthopedist who performed follow-up care.

One month later, while preparing a settlement package, Barnabas discovers a chart note from a free Christian medical clinic he visited the day after the fall (his daughter''s idea because regular offices were closed for the weekend). A visiting retired missionary physician, Dr. Paul, examined the leg, noted significant swelling and tenderness, and wrote that the mechanism was consistent with a fall from height and that X-rays should be considered. The defendant has received no other information about Dr. Paul or this visit.

A: Wait until trial and disclose the third physician only if the defendant objects

B: Nothing, because discovery responses are judged only when served

C: Timely supplement or correct the response unless the information has otherwise been made known to the defendant

D: Supplement only if the defendant serves a second interrogatory asking for updates', 'What must Barnabas do?',
