    'A devout Christian mother of the bride, Martha, sues two wedding vendors in federal court under diversity jurisdiction. Martha is a Texas citizen; both vendors are California citizens. The complaint alleges that the florist, Daniel, breached the floral arrangement contract by delivering the wrong color of flowers for the wedding reception, and that the disc jockey service, owned and operated by Hannah, breached its contract by playing explicit music during the reception. After discovery, the district court dismisses all of Martha''s claims against Daniel on the ground that they are barred by the contract''s one-year limitations clause, but Martha''s claims against Hannah remain pending. The court does not mention Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 54(b) and does not direct the entry of final judgment.', 'A devout Christian mother of the bride, Martha, sues two wedding vendors in federal court under diversity jurisdiction. Martha is a Texas citizen; both vendors are California citizens. The complaint alleges that the florist, Daniel, breached the floral arrangement contract by delivering the wrong color of flowers for the wedding reception, and that the disc jockey service, owned and operated by Hannah, breached its contract by playing explicit music during the reception. After discovery, the district court dismisses all of Martha''s claims against Daniel on the ground that they are barred by the contract''s one-year limitations clause, but Martha''s claims against Hannah remain pending. The court does not mention Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 54(b) and does not direct the entry of final judgment.', 'May Martha immediately appeal the dismissal of Daniel as a final judgment?',
    NULL, 'draft', 'c3_transformed', 1, '{"source_qid": "19169", "schema_variant": "transformed_v1", "outline_code": "93110200", "transformed": true, "c3_confidence": "ANCHOR_ASSISTED", "c3_verdict": "PASS"}'
