    'Mary owns Five Loaves Press, a private company that prints Bible-verse memory-card kits for homeschool groups. Ruth sues Five Loaves Press in federal court over a ruined Lent-season print run. Before the complaint was filed, Five Loaves Press knew litigation was likely and its lawyer told the company to preserve project records. The company kept using a project-board app that automatically erased production notes every 14 days. Several ''manna batch'' notes about the disputed print run disappeared and cannot be restored from backups or replaced through other discovery. Ruth shows that losing the notes prejudices her proof about when the print run failed. The record shows careless preservation, but not a deliberate plan to keep the notes from Ruth in the lawsuit.', 'Mary owns Five Loaves Press, a private company that prints Bible-verse memory-card kits for homeschool groups. Ruth sues Five Loaves Press in federal court over a ruined Lent-season print run. Before the complaint was filed, Five Loaves Press knew litigation was likely and its lawyer told the company to preserve project records. The company kept using a project-board app that automatically erased production notes every 14 days. Several ''manna batch'' notes about the disputed print run disappeared and cannot be restored from backups or replaced through other discovery. Ruth shows that losing the notes prejudices her proof about when the print run failed. The record shows careless preservation, but not a deliberate plan to keep the notes from Ruth in the lawsuit.', 'Which sanction framework should the court use?',
