    'Hannah, a wedding photographer, agreed in writing to photograph Sarah and Joel''s wedding ceremony and reception for a flat fee. After the wedding, Sarah and Joel sued Hannah in federal court for breach of contract, alleging that Hannah failed to deliver a promised set of edited photographs and a printed wedding album. Hannah moved for summary judgment, arguing that the contract plainly required only an online gallery. The district judge denied the motion and added in a footnote: "This is an important issue." The judge did not enter any order certifying the question for interlocutory appeal under 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b), and the court of appeals did not grant Hannah permission to appeal. Hannah immediately filed a notice of appeal, claiming that Section 1292(b) entitles her to an appeal as of right.', 'Hannah, a wedding photographer, agreed in writing to photograph Sarah and Joel''s wedding ceremony and reception for a flat fee. After the wedding, Sarah and Joel sued Hannah in federal court for breach of contract, alleging that Hannah failed to deliver a promised set of edited photographs and a printed wedding album. Hannah moved for summary judgment, arguing that the contract plainly required only an online gallery. The district judge denied the motion and added in a footnote: "This is an important issue." The judge did not enter any order certifying the question for interlocutory appeal under 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b), and the court of appeals did not grant Hannah permission to appeal. Hannah immediately filed a notice of appeal, claiming that Section 1292(b) entitles her to an appeal as of right.', 'Is Hannah correct?',
    NULL, 'draft', 'c3_transformed', 1, '{"source_qid": "19337", "schema_variant": "transformed_v1", "outline_code": "93110100", "transformed": true, "c3_confidence": "HARD_STRUCTURAL", "c3_verdict": "PASS"}'
) ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE
