    '20038_vessel_refit', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Pretrial Procedures', 'Counterclaims',
    'Luke, a skilled boatbuilder, sues Cornelius for the unpaid balance on a contract to refit Cornelius''s commercial fishing vessel, including substantial hull repairs and replacement rigging. Cornelius has a related claim that the refit work was defective in ways that render the vessel unseaworthy and dangerous to operate. Full relief on that defective-refit claim, however, requires joining the naval architect who prepared the detailed specifications for the work. That architect now lives in Norway and is not subject to the personal jurisdiction of the court.

**A.** No, because all defective-refit claims are permissive counterclaims.

**B.** Yes, because it arises from the same vessel refit project.

**C.** No, if adjudicating the claim requires joining a party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction.

**D.** Yes, because counterclaims never require personal jurisdiction over additional parties.', 'Luke, a skilled boatbuilder, sues Cornelius for the unpaid balance on a contract to refit Cornelius''s commercial fishing vessel, including substantial hull repairs and replacement rigging. Cornelius has a related claim that the refit work was defective in ways that render the vessel unseaworthy and dangerous to operate. Full relief on that defective-refit claim, however, requires joining the naval architect who prepared the detailed specifications for the work. That architect now lives in Norway and is not subject to the personal jurisdiction of the court.

**A.** No, because all defective-refit claims are permissive counterclaims.

**B.** Yes, because it arises from the same vessel refit project.

**C.** No, if adjudicating the claim requires joining a party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction.

**D.** Yes, because counterclaims never require personal jurisdiction over additional parties.', 'Is Cornelius''s related defective-refit claim necessarily a compulsory counterclaim?',
