) VALUES (
    '22756_hannahs-conference-verdict', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Verdicts and Judgments', 'Judgments and Verdicts',
    'After a jury trial in federal district court based on diversity jurisdiction, Hannah, a Christian author and speaker from State A, obtained a $1,250,000 compensatory damages verdict against a State B driver whose car crossed the center line and struck her vehicle while she was en route to lead a retreat for women in ministry. The accident occurred in State B. The federal judge determined that the award was excessive under the standard applied in State B, the place of the accident. The judge offered Hannah the option of accepting a lower amount or proceeding to a new trial on damages. May the court do so?

A: No, because forcing the plaintiff to accept a potentially lower award violates the Seventh Amendment.

B: Yes, because the court believed the award was excessive under the state standard.

C: No, because there was no indication the jury''s award was so excessive as to “shock the conscience.”

D: Yes, because the court could simply lower the jury''s award if it chose to do so.', 'After a jury trial in federal district court based on diversity jurisdiction, Hannah, a Christian author and speaker from State A, obtained a $1,250,000 compensatory damages verdict against a State B driver whose car crossed the center line and struck her vehicle while she was en route to lead a retreat for women in ministry. The accident occurred in State B. The federal judge determined that the award was excessive under the standard applied in State B, the place of the accident. The judge offered Hannah the option of accepting a lower amount or proceeding to a new trial on damages. May the court do so?

A: No, because forcing the plaintiff to accept a potentially lower award violates the Seventh Amendment.

B: Yes, because the court believed the award was excessive under the state standard.

C: No, because there was no indication the jury''s award was so excessive as to “shock the conscience.”

D: Yes, because the court could simply lower the jury''s award if it chose to do so.', 'May the court do so?',
