    '22863_worship_leader_tortious_interference', 'CIVIL_PROCEDURE', 'Jurisdiction and Venue', 'Federal Subject-Matter Jurisdiction',
    'Caleb, a worship leader and a citizen of State A, was a close spiritual protégé of Margaret, an elderly church patron who owned property in several states. When Margaret died, Caleb filed a probate claim in state court challenging Margaret''s last will and testament, which left her entire estate to her adult son, David, a citizen of State B. Caleb claimed that Margaret had promised to leave him money to provide for his financial security. At the same time, Caleb filed a tortious interference claim against David in federal court, seeking $5 million in damages, alleging that David had purposely kept Margaret away from Caleb during the last year of Margaret''s life to prevent any change to the will. After a trial on the merits, the federal court ruled in favor of Caleb on the tortious interference claim. Thereafter, the state court found the will to be valid. David appealed the federal court''s decision, arguing that the federal court lacked subject matter jurisdiction over Caleb''s claim because the claim involved probate matters.', 'Caleb, a worship leader and a citizen of State A, was a close spiritual protégé of Margaret, an elderly church patron who owned property in several states. When Margaret died, Caleb filed a probate claim in state court challenging Margaret''s last will and testament, which left her entire estate to her adult son, David, a citizen of State B. Caleb claimed that Margaret had promised to leave him money to provide for his financial security. At the same time, Caleb filed a tortious interference claim against David in federal court, seeking $5 million in damages, alleging that David had purposely kept Margaret away from Caleb during the last year of Margaret''s life to prevent any change to the will. After a trial on the merits, the federal court ruled in favor of Caleb on the tortious interference claim. Thereafter, the state court found the will to be valid. David appealed the federal court''s decision, arguing that the federal court lacked subject matter jurisdiction over Caleb''s claim because the claim involved probate matters.', 'Does the federal court have subject matter jurisdiction over the tortious interference claim?',
