) VALUES (
    '14226_corinth-wards', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Individual Rights', 'Voting Rights / Fifteenth Amendment',
    'In the historic city of Corinth, twenty percent of the residents are members of minority racial groups. These residents live evenly throughout the neighborhoods surrounding the old town square and the new riverfront developments. The five members of the city council are elected from five single-member wards of nearly equal population. No one from these minority racial groups has ever been elected to the city council. Lydia, a seller of purple goods, and Priscilla, along with other members of the Riverside Prayer Fellowship who belong to these minority racial groups, file suit in federal district court. They seek a declaratory judgment that the ward system is unconstitutional because it diminishes the ability of voters who are members of minority racial groups to affect the outcome of the city elections. They ask the court to order the city to adopt an at-large election system in which the five candidates receiving the greatest number of votes would be elected. No state or federal statutes apply to this suit. Which of the following constitutional provisions provides the most obvious basis for the plaintiffs'' claim?', 'In the historic city of Corinth, twenty percent of the residents are members of minority racial groups. These residents live evenly throughout the neighborhoods surrounding the old town square and the new riverfront developments. The five members of the city council are elected from five single-member wards of nearly equal population. No one from these minority racial groups has ever been elected to the city council. Lydia, a seller of purple goods, and Priscilla, along with other members of the Riverside Prayer Fellowship who belong to these minority racial groups, file suit in federal district court. They seek a declaratory judgment that the ward system is unconstitutional because it diminishes the ability of voters who are members of minority racial groups to affect the outcome of the city elections. They ask the court to order the city to adopt an at-large election system in which the five candidates receiving the greatest number of votes would be elected. No state or federal statutes apply to this suit. Which of the following constitutional provisions provides the most obvious basis for the plaintiffs'' claim?', 'Which of the following constitutional provisions provides the most obvious basis for the plaintiffs'' claim in this suit?',
