    '14248_lydia_clay_reclamation', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Eminent domain—Takings Clause', 'Individual Rights',
    'Lydia''s Workshop, LLC, a private Christian craft company, bought a broad desert parcel that contained a vein of fine clay. Lydia planned to quarry the clay and make Scripture-themed tiles for sale. Before digging began, the company bought portable quarry equipment and kiln trailers. Congress then enacted a statute imposing new aquifer-protection buffers and land-reclamation requirements on all commercial clay-extraction operations in the United States. The statute made Lydia''s planned clay operation economically infeasible. Lydia''s Workshop sold the parcel to Ruth, who uses it as an olive farm. The sale price allowed Lydia''s Workshop to recover its original investment in the land, but it did not cover the cost of the quarry equipment and kiln trailers or the profits Lydia expected to earn from the tile business. In an action filed against the appropriate federal official, Lydia''s Workshop claims that the statute effected a taking for which it is entitled to just compensation equal to the equipment costs and expected profits.', 'Lydia''s Workshop, LLC, a private Christian craft company, bought a broad desert parcel that contained a vein of fine clay. Lydia planned to quarry the clay and make Scripture-themed tiles for sale. Before digging began, the company bought portable quarry equipment and kiln trailers. Congress then enacted a statute imposing new aquifer-protection buffers and land-reclamation requirements on all commercial clay-extraction operations in the United States. The statute made Lydia''s planned clay operation economically infeasible. Lydia''s Workshop sold the parcel to Ruth, who uses it as an olive farm. The sale price allowed Lydia''s Workshop to recover its original investment in the land, but it did not cover the cost of the quarry equipment and kiln trailers or the profits Lydia expected to earn from the tile business. In an action filed against the appropriate federal official, Lydia''s Workshop claims that the statute effected a taki