    '14339_good_shepherd_science_kit', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'The Federal-State Relationship', 'Relations Between Federal and State Governments',
    'Lydia''s Little Shepherd Workshop has its headquarters and only production room in one state. It makes a children''s tabletop learning kit called "The Good Shepherd Trail," in which children move small wooden sheep through hills, wells, and a sheepfold while learning basic animal-care facts. The kit accurately depicts common grazing conditions, sheep behavior, and simple shepherding equipment. It is high quality, safe, durable, and has sold very well.

Several other kit makers, all located outside the state, have developed lower-priced Good Shepherd-style kits. Those makers have contracts to sell their kits to homeschool stores and toy stores in the state. Their kits are also safe and durable, but they depict grazing conditions and sheep behavior less realistically than Lydia''s kit. Because of the price difference, sales of those outside kits have severely reduced sales of Lydia''s in-state kit.

The state legislature recently enacted a law "to protect the children of this state from faulty animal science and to protect in-state learning-kit makers from unfair competition." The law forbids the sale in the state of any children''s learning kit that purports to represent shepherding or sheep care and does not satisfy specified animal-science criteria. Lydia''s kit satisfies all of the criteria; none of the competing outside kits satisfy them.', 'Lydia''s Little Shepherd Workshop has its headquarters and only production room in one state. It makes a children''s tabletop learning kit called "The Good Shepherd Trail," in which children move small wooden sheep through hills, wells, and a sheepfold while learning basic animal-care facts. The kit accurately depicts common grazing conditions, sheep behavior, and simple shepherding equipment. It is high quality, safe, durable, and has sold very well.

Several other kit makers, all located outside the state, have developed lower-priced Good Shepherd-style kits. Those makers have contracts to sell their kits to homeschool stores and toy stores in the state. Their kits are also safe and durable, but they depict grazing conditions and sheep behavior less realistically than Lydia''s kit. Because of the price difference, sales of those outside kits have severely reduced sales of Lydia''s in-state kit.

The state legislature recently enacted a law "to protect the children of this state from faulty animal science and to protect in-state learning-kit makers from unfair competition." The law forbids the sale in the state of any children''s learning kit that purports to represent shepherding or sheep care and does not satisfy specified animal-science criteria. Lydia''s kit satisfies all of the criteria; none of the competing outside kits satisfy them.', 'Is the state law constitutional?',
