    '19194_loaves_pages', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Equal Protection', 'Rational basis — sexual orientation / federal law / equal dignity',
    'A federal statute provides that, for every federal tax, employee-benefit, grant, and insurance program, the word ''spouse'' means only a husband or wife of the opposite sex, and ''marriage'' means only a legal union between one man and one woman. Stephen and Barnabas, who run a little gospel-poster shop called Loaves & Pages, are legally married under their state’s law. After Stephen takes a federal park-service job, he tries to enroll Barnabas for spousal health coverage available to opposite-sex spouses of federal employees. The agency denies the enrollment solely because Barnabas is not an opposite-sex spouse. Stephen and Barnabas challenge the statute under the Equal Protection component of the Fifth Amendment Due Process Clause.', 'A federal statute provides that, for every federal tax, employee-benefit, grant, and insurance program, the word ''spouse'' means only a husband or wife of the opposite sex, and ''marriage'' means only a legal union between one man and one woman. Stephen and Barnabas, who run a little gospel-poster shop called Loaves & Pages, are legally married under their state’s law. After Stephen takes a federal park-service job, he tries to enroll Barnabas for spousal health coverage available to opposite-sex spouses of federal employees. The agency denies the enrollment solely because Barnabas is not an opposite-sex spouse. Stephen and Barnabas challenge the statute under the Equal Protection component of the Fifth Amendment Due Process Clause.', 'Under Equal Protection analysis, how should a court evaluate the federal statute’s refusal to recognize their state-law marriage for federal benefits?',
