    'The State of Galilee funds its public schools with a statewide foundation grant plus whatever each local district raises through local property taxes. In Cana Hills, a lakeside district with expensive homes and commercial property, the public schools spend about $22,400 per student each year. In Bethany Flats, an old cannery district with a small tax base, the public schools spend about $7,300 per student each year. Mary, Peter, and Lydia, parents of children in Bethany Flats, bring an Equal Protection challenge. They argue that the funding plan denies their children an equal public education. The state responds that the plan preserves local control of schools and rationally connects local tax revenue to local school spending.', 'The State of Galilee funds its public schools with a statewide foundation grant plus whatever each local district raises through local property taxes. In Cana Hills, a lakeside district with expensive homes and commercial property, the public schools spend about $22,400 per student each year. In Bethany Flats, an old cannery district with a small tax base, the public schools spend about $7,300 per student each year. Mary, Peter, and Lydia, parents of children in Bethany Flats, bring an Equal Protection challenge. They argue that the funding plan denies their children an equal public education. The state responds that the plan preserves local control of schools and rationally connects local tax revenue to local school spending.', 'Under Equal Protection analysis, which standard of review applies, and how should the court likely rule?',
