    '19638_v2', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Separation of Powers', 'Veto',
    'Timothy and Lydia are the parents of a 10-year-old boy, Daniel. Daniel was diagnosed with acute leukemia. His oncologists told the family that without a bone-marrow transplant, Daniel would likely die within months. The parents are devout Christians who believe that prayer and anointing with oil alone should be used for healing. They refused to consent to the transplant. The hospital petitioned a state court, which held a hearing. Daniel''s doctors testified that the transplant had a strong chance of saving his life and that Daniel would likely die without it. The court entered an order authorizing the transplant over the parents'' objection. The parents argue that substantive due process gives them an absolute veto over medical decisions for their child.', 'Timothy and Lydia are the parents of a 10-year-old boy, Daniel. Daniel was diagnosed with acute leukemia. His oncologists told the family that without a bone-marrow transplant, Daniel would likely die within months. The parents are devout Christians who believe that prayer and anointing with oil alone should be used for healing. They refused to consent to the transplant. The hospital petitioned a state court, which held a hearing. Daniel''s doctors testified that the transplant had a strong chance of saving his life and that Daniel would likely die without it. The court entered an order authorizing the transplant over the parents'' objection. The parents argue that substantive due process gives them an absolute veto over medical decisions for their child.', 'Is the court order constitutional?',
