) VALUES (
    '22611_state-id-church-roster', 'CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW', 'Judicial Review', 'Constitutional limitations and justiciability',
    'A state church maintains a "Community Participation Roster" that lists every member who attends worship services, participates in Bible studies, or volunteers for church outreach programs. The roster is kept in the church office and is available for review by church leaders. A member of the congregation, David, learns of the roster''s existence and files suit in federal court against the church''s session (governing board), seeking an injunction to stop the practice. David alleges that because nearly every active church member appears on the roster, the compilation of this information has a chilling effect on the exercise of his rights under the First Amendment to the United States Constitution — specifically, his rights to free exercise of religion and freedom of association. David has never been denied any church benefit, disciplined, or threatened with any adverse action because of the roster. What is the clearest reason for the dismissal of David''s suit?

**Answer A:** The creation of a community participation roster by a private church involves the resolution of a political question.

**Answer B:** The action is unripe.

**Answer C:** Under the Eleventh Amendment to the United States Constitution, the church''s governing board is immune to lawsuits of this kind.

**Answer D:** The question presented is moot.', 'A state church maintains a "Community Participation Roster" that lists every member who attends worship services, participates in Bible studies, or volunteers for church outreach programs. The roster is kept in the church office and is available for review by church leaders. A member of the congregation, David, learns of the roster''s existence and files suit in federal court against the church''s session (governing board), seeking an injunction to stop the practice. David alleges that because nearly every active church member appears on the roster, the compilation of this information has a chilling effect on the exercise of his rights under the First Amendment to the United States Constitution — specifically, his rights to free exercise of religion and freedom of association. David has never been denied any church benefit, disciplined, or threatened with any adverse action because of the roster. What is the clearest reason for the dismissal of David''s suit?

