    'For decades, State A prohibited mobile coffee carts from operating inside bookstores. Lydia, the owner of a private Christian bookstore and coffee bar, formed a neighborhood group called Open the Manna Carts and urged the State A legislature to legalize and regulate bookstore coffee carts so local shops could compete with similar shops in State B. At committee hearings, Lydia presented testimony about lost sales to State B shops, the economic effect on local bookstores, and changing public attitudes toward small in-store coffee service. During the same hearings, Lydia admitted that she had quietly run an unlicensed mobile coffee cart in the rear reading room of her store for several years.

The legislature then enacted a law allowing and regulating mobile coffee carts in bookstores. The law also included a provision stating that Lydia''s food-service permit would be suspended for two years because she had violated the prior coffee-cart ban in her bookstore. Lydia challenged the new law in federal court as unconstitutional.', 'For decades, State A prohibited mobile coffee carts from operating inside bookstores. Lydia, the owner of a private Christian bookstore and coffee bar, formed a neighborhood group called Open the Manna Carts and urged the State A legislature to legalize and regulate bookstore coffee carts so local shops could compete with similar shops in State B. At committee hearings, Lydia presented testimony about lost sales to State B shops, the economic effect on local bookstores, and changing public attitudes toward small in-store coffee service. During the same hearings, Lydia admitted that she had quietly run an unlicensed mobile coffee cart in the rear reading room of her store for several years.

