    '14378_prayer_journal_discount', 'CONTRACTS', 'Parol Evidence Rule', 'Contract Content and Meaning',
    'Barnabas sells custom prayer journals to Lydia, who runs a private Christian bookstore. During the last five years, they have made hundreds of separate journal-supply contracts. Each time Barnabas delivered a shipment, Lydia signed an invoice listing the agreed price for that shipment. The invoices never mentioned any discount for early payment.

In the devotional-goods wholesale trade, the regular custom is to allow a 2% discount from the invoice price if payment is made within 10 days after delivery. In every prior transaction between Barnabas and Lydia, Lydia paid 15 days after delivery and deducted 5% from the invoice price. Barnabas accepted those payments without objection.

Barnabas and Lydia recently entered into a contract for one shipment of leather-bound study journals at a price of $84,000. They used the same delivery procedure and the same invoice form as before. Barnabas delivered the journals, and Lydia signed the invoice. Three days after delivery, Lydia received this note from Barnabas: "Payment in full in accordance with the signed invoice is due immediately. No discounts permitted. s/Barnabas."', 'Barnabas sells custom prayer journals to Lydia, who runs a private Christian bookstore. During the last five years, they have made hundreds of separate journal-supply contracts. Each time Barnabas delivered a shipment, Lydia signed an invoice listing the agreed price for that shipment. The invoices never mentioned any discount for early payment.

In the devotional-goods wholesale trade, the regular custom is to allow a 2% discount from the invoice price if payment is made within 10 days after delivery. In every prior transaction between Barnabas and Lydia, Lydia paid 15 days after delivery and deducted 5% from the invoice price. Barnabas accepted those payments without objection.

