    '14385_esther_engraver', 'CONTRACTS', 'Gap-Fillers, Interpretation, and the Parol Evidence Rule > Parol Evidence Rule', 'Contract Content and Meaning',
    'Esther bought a new laser engraver from Timothy''s Artisan Supply for her private Scripture-bookmark business. The written contract stated that the price of the engraver was $28,600 and that Esther would receive an "exchange credit of $9,300 for Esther''s old engraver." The old engraver had recently been damaged when a storage-room sprinkler failed. The contract contained a merger clause stating: "This writing is the complete and exclusive statement of the parties'' agreement, and no other agreements or understandings are part of this sale." When Esther picked up the new engraver, she delivered the old engraver to Timothy''s Artisan Supply. At that time, Timothy''s claimed that the exchange credit included an assignment of Esther''s claim against her insurance company for the damage to the old engraver. Esther refused to assign the claim. Timothy''s sued Esther to recover the insurance payment. Timothy''s has offered evidence that the parties agreed during negotiations that Timothy''s would be entitled to that insurance payment.', 'Esther bought a new laser engraver from Timothy''s Artisan Supply for her private Scripture-bookmark business. The written contract stated that the price of the engraver was $28,600 and that Esther would receive an "exchange credit of $9,300 for Esther''s old engraver." The old engraver had recently been damaged when a storage-room sprinkler failed. The contract contained a merger clause stating: "This writing is the complete and exclusive statement of the parties'' agreement, and no other agreements or understandings are part of this sale." When Esther picked up the new engraver, she delivered the old engraver to Timothy''s Artisan Supply. At that time, Timothy''s claimed that the exchange credit included an assignment of Esther''s claim against her insurance company for the damage to the old engraver. Esther refused to assign the claim. Timothy''s sued Esther to recover the insurance payment. Timothy''s has offered evidence that the parties agreed during negotiations that Timothy''s would be entitled to that insurance payment.', 'Should the court admit this evidence?',
