    'Lydia owned an antique set of handbells that she used for neighborhood Easter concerts. She paid Barnabas Protection Plans for a written equipment-protection agreement covering accidental damage to the handbells. The agreement omitted coverage disclosures that a state service-plan statute requires for agreements of that kind. During a Good Friday rehearsal, a riser collapsed and damaged the handbells, a loss covered by the agreement’s stated terms. Barnabas refused payment, arguing that its omission made the agreement unenforceable because the agreement violated the statute.', 'Lydia owned an antique set of handbells that she used for neighborhood Easter concerts. She paid Barnabas Protection Plans for a written equipment-protection agreement covering accidental damage to the handbells. The agreement omitted coverage disclosures that a state service-plan statute requires for agreements of that kind. During a Good Friday rehearsal, a riser collapsed and damaged the handbells, a loss covered by the agreement’s stated terms. Barnabas refused payment, arguing that its omission made the agreement unenforceable because the agreement violated the statute.', 'Is Lydia likely to recover for the loss?',
