    'Hannah, a devout widow and longtime leader in her small rural church''s women''s Bible study and prayer group, inherited several thousand acres of timberland from her late husband, a faithful deacon who had always managed the family''s modest logging income. With several children still at home, high expenses for their Christian homeschool co-op tuition and curriculum, plus her commitment to fund a family-supported short-term mission trip to construct housing for widows in a developing country, Hannah found herself land-rich but cash-poor after unexpected medical bills. Pressed for funds to meet the mission pledge and family needs, she advertised the proposed sale of harvest and removal rights for standing timber on a choice 2,000-acre tract of mature trees. The only response was an offer by Simon, the owner of a large, integrated commercial logging and lumber enterprise, after inspection of the advertised tract. Simon offered to buy, cut, and remove the standing timber from the advertised tract at a cash price 70% lower than the regionally prevailing price for comparable timber harvest rights. Hannah, by then in desperate financial straits and knowing little about current timber values (her husband had handled all the business details), signed and delivered to Simon a letter accepting the offer. If, before Simon commences cutting, Hannah''s family fortunes suddenly improve through an unexpected large love offering from her church community and a rebound in her late husband''s investment accounts, and she wishes to get out of the timber harvest deal with Simon, which of the following legal concepts affords her best prospect of effective cancellation?', 'Hannah, a devout widow and longtime leader in her small rural church''s women''s Bible study and prayer group, inherited several thousand acres of timberland from her late husband, a faithful deacon who had always managed the family''s modest logging income. With several children still at home, high expenses for 