INSERT INTO questions (
    external_id, subject, topic, subtopic,
    fact_pattern, question_stem, call_of_question,
    difficulty, status, source_type, version, metadata
) VALUES (
    '14400_mechanical_camel', 'CONTRACTS', 'Defenses to Enforceability', 'Mistake / Lack of Mutual Assent',
    'A contract may be rescinded for mistake when the mistaken fact existed at contracting, went to a basic assumption of the bargain, materially harmed the party seeking rescission, and was not a risk that party bore.

**Pass-1 Step 2 — invariant layer preserved.**
The issue remains buyer-side rescission for mistake. The credited outcome remains the hidden, undiscoverable, main-use defect. The dominant trap remains the pedigree/authenticity paperwork mistake. The call remains a “which scenario best supports buyer rather than seller rescission” call. The answer-array geometry remains a 3-vs-1 scenario set: three post-sale surprises fail because they do not support buyer-side mistake rescission; one hidden main-use defect survives.

**Pass-1 Step 5 — locked final Christian variation.**

Mary, who knew nothing about stage machinery, inherited a motorized camel prop from her late uncle’s attic. The camel had an annoying habit of jerking and startling children during rehearsals. Mary sold the camel for $2,400 to Daniel, an experienced designer of touring Christmas pageants, who knew that Mary was ignorant about stage machinery. Before the sale, Mary told Daniel, “I hate to say it, but this camel jerks around and is nothing special.”', 'A contract may be rescinded for mistake when the mistaken fact existed at contracting, went to a basic assumption of the bargain, materially harmed the party seeking rescission, and was not a risk that party bore.

**Pass-1 Step 2 — invariant layer preserved.**
The issue remains buyer-side rescission for mistake. The credited outcome remains the hidden, undiscoverable, main-use defect. The dominant trap remains the pedigree/authenticity paperwork mistake. The call remains a “which scenario best supports buyer rather than seller rescission” call. The answer-array geometry remains a 3-vs-1 scenario set: three post-sale surprises fail because they do not support buyer-side mistake rescission; one hidden main-use defect survives.

