) VALUES (
    '14401_psalm_van_repair', 'CONTRACTS', 'Defenses to Enforceability', 'Statute of Frauds',
    'A suretyship promise is within the Statute of Frauds, but it is enforceable if there is a signed memorandum by the party to be charged that identifies the debt, the parties, and the essential terms. The memorandum can be informal, can be made after the oral promise, and need not be addressed to the promisee.

**Pass-1 Step 2 - invariant layer preserved.**

The issue remains a guarantor''s statute-of-frauds defense to a promise to pay another person''s debt. The credited outcome remains that the defense fails because the guarantor later signed an informal writing that adequately memorialized the guaranty. The dominant trap remains the "the guaranty was oral, so the defense wins" answer. The array keeps the same two-no/two-yes geometry: two choices say the defense fails, two say it succeeds, and only one choice gives the right reason.

**Pass-1 Step 5 - locked final Christian variation.**

Matthew ran a private Psalm-calligraphy booth and needed his delivery van repaired before a Christian craft fair. Peter, who owned a small repair garage, agreed to complete the $3,600 repair on credit only if Matthew''s aunt Ruth would guarantee payment. At Matthew''s suggestion, Peter telephoned Ruth, explained the repair credit, and asked whether she would guarantee Matthew''s bill. Ruth replied, "Yes. Repair Matthew''s van, and I will pay the $3,600 if he does not."

Peter completed the repairs, and Matthew orally agreed to pay Peter in eight weeks. The next day, Ruth sent Matthew a handwritten note that concluded, "Matthew, I was glad to help by promising Peter that I would pay your eight-week $3,600 van-repair bill if you do not. /s/ Ruth."

Matthew did not pay when the bill came due, and Ruth also refused to pay. Peter sued Ruth for breach of contract. Ruth raised the statute of frauds as her only defense.', 'A suretyship promise is within the Statute of Frauds, but it is enforceable if there is a signed memorandum by the party to be charged that identifies the debt, the parties, and the essential terms. The memorandum can be informal, can be made after the oral promise, and need not be addressed to the promisee.

