    '14404_ruth_naomi_nativity_cases', 'CONTRACTS', 'Modifications', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge',
    'Under UCC Article 2, an agreement modifying a contract for the sale of goods needs no new consideration to be binding.

**Pass-1 Step 2 - invariant layer preserved.**
The issue remains whether a seller is contractually bound to deliver extra goods added by a later signed offer and timely acceptance. The credited outcome remains that the seller is bound because the parties effectively modified the original goods-sale contract. The dominant trap remains the claim that the buyer supplied new bargained-for consideration merely by agreeing to take the added goods. The answer array remains one true modification answer against three wrong reasons: new-consideration trap, waiver-neighbor trap, and rescission misfire.

**Pass-1 Step 5 - locked final Christian variation.**

On March 3, Naomi and Ruth contracted for the sale by Naomi to Ruth of a hand-carved nativity display that Naomi had inherited from her grandmother. Ruth agreed to pay the purchase price of $6,800 when Naomi delivered the display on April 2.

On March 12, Ruth received a signed note from Naomi that stated: "I have decided to dispose of the padded travel cases holding the nativity display you have already purchased. If you want the cases, I will deliver them to you along with the display on April 2 at no additional cost to you. Let me know before March 18 whether you want them. I will not sell them to anyone else before then."

On March 16, Ruth emailed Naomi the following message, and Naomi received it that day: "I accept your offer of the cases." Naomi was not a merchant with respect to either nativity displays or padded travel cases.', 'Under UCC Article 2, an agreement modifying a contract for the sale of goods needs no new consideration to be binding.

