    '14416_worship_sound_booth', 'CONTRACTS', 'Interpretation', 'Contract Content and Meaning',
    'Lydia, who had no previous experience running a concert venue or hiring professional sound companies, bought a small private Christian concert hall. She signed a nine-month agreement with Stephen''s sound company for the hall''s sound-booth services. The agreement provided for "daily booth checks at $900 a week." During negotiations, Stephen knew that Lydia expected the sound company to check the booth seven days a week. At trial, clear evidence showed that in the professional sound-service trade, "daily booth checks" did not include Sunday checks. After Stephen''s company skipped two Sundays in a row and said it would never perform Sunday checks, Lydia canceled the agreement. Stephen''s company sued Lydia for breach of contract.', 'Lydia, who had no previous experience running a concert venue or hiring professional sound companies, bought a small private Christian concert hall. She signed a nine-month agreement with Stephen''s sound company for the hall''s sound-booth services. The agreement provided for "daily booth checks at $900 a week." During negotiations, Stephen knew that Lydia expected the sound company to check the booth seven days a week. At trial, clear evidence showed that in the professional sound-service trade, "daily booth checks" did not include Sunday checks. After Stephen''s company skipped two Sundays in a row and said it would never perform Sunday checks, Lydia canceled the agreement. Stephen''s company sued Lydia for breach of contract.', 'Is Stephen''s company likely to succeed in its action?',
