    '14422_scripture_journal_arbitration', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Acceptance',
    'Martha, who operated a private Christian retreat-supply store, sent Paul, who ran a commercial bindery, an offer to buy 300 leather-bound Scripture journals with a specified paper weight and cover finish. The offer contained no terms except the number of journals and the required paper and cover quality.

Paul emailed an order acknowledgment. The acknowledgment repeated Martha''s quantity and quality terms and stated that shipment would occur within four days. Tucked among 14 standard printed terms in the acknowledgment was a clause stating that any dispute about the journals'' quality would be submitted to arbitration. Neither Martha nor Paul said anything further about arbitration.

Paul shipped the journals, and Martha accepted them. A dispute later arose between Martha and Paul about the journals'' paper quality. Paul asserted that the dispute had to be submitted to arbitration. Martha instead sued Paul in court. In that suit, which of the following arguments best supports Paul''s position that Martha must submit the dispute to arbitration?', 'Martha, who operated a private Christian retreat-supply store, sent Paul, who ran a commercial bindery, an offer to buy 300 leather-bound Scripture journals with a specified paper weight and cover finish. The offer contained no terms except the number of journals and the required paper and cover quality.

Paul emailed an order acknowledgment. The acknowledgment repeated Martha''s quantity and quality terms and stated that shipment would occur within four days. Tucked among 14 standard printed terms in the acknowledgment was a clause stating that any dispute about the journals'' quality would be submitted to arbitration. Neither Martha nor Paul said anything further about arbitration.

Paul shipped the journals, and Martha accepted them. A dispute later arose between Martha and Paul about the journals'' paper quality. Paul asserted that the dispute had to be submitted to arbitration. Martha instead sued Paul in court. In that suit, which of the following arguments best supports Paul''s position that Martha must submit the dispute to arbitration?', 'In that suit, which of the following arguments best supports Paul''s position that Martha must submit the dispute to arbitration?',
