    '14424_christmas_pageant_lantern_bid', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Promissory Estoppel',
    'Esther, a general event contractor, was preparing a sealed bid for Lydia''s private Christmas Pageant Pavilion. Paul, a lighting installer, sent Esther this signed email: ''Will install all overhead lantern trusses and speaker wiring for the pavilion, per Lydia''s specifications, for $32,000 if you accept within a reasonable time after the prime contract is awarded. /s/ Paul.'' Ruth, Stephen, and Barnabas sent similar sub-bids of $40,000, $42,000, and $44,000. Esther used Paul''s $32,000 figure in preparing and submitting her own sealed bid to Lydia. Before the bids were opened, Paul truthfully told Esther that his sub-bid had been based on an $8,000 computational error and was therefore revoked. Shortly afterward, Lydia awarded Esther the pavilion contract. Esther then contracted with Ruth to do the lighting work for $40,000. Esther sues Paul to recover $8,000.', 'Esther, a general event contractor, was preparing a sealed bid for Lydia''s private Christmas Pageant Pavilion. Paul, a lighting installer, sent Esther this signed email: ''Will install all overhead lantern trusses and speaker wiring for the pavilion, per Lydia''s specifications, for $32,000 if you accept within a reasonable time after the prime contract is awarded. /s/ Paul.'' Ruth, Stephen, and Barnabas sent similar sub-bids of $40,000, $42,000, and $44,000. Esther used Paul''s $32,000 figure in preparing and submitting her own sealed bid to Lydia. Before the bids were opened, Paul truthfully told Esther that his sub-bid had been based on an $8,000 computational error and was therefore revoked. Shortly afterward, Lydia awarded Esther the pavilion contract. Esther then contracted with Ruth to do the lighting work for $40,000. Esther sues Paul to recover $8,000.', 'Which of the following, if proved, would most strengthen Esther''s prospect of recovery?',
