    'After a few rounds of bargaining, Daniel, who operated a shop selling premium handbell sets, knew that Hannah wanted a new set for her nephew''s hymn-themed wedding procession. On March 10, Daniel sent Hannah a signed, dated note saying, ''If by Friday we land on the same handbell set and price, are we agreed?'' Hannah wrote ''Yes,'' signed below it, and returned the note on March 12. On March 14, Hannah mailed Daniel a signed letter offering to buy ''one new five-octave handbell set, with all carrying cases and accessories, for $24,600 cash on delivery not later than April 15.'' By coincidence, Daniel mailed Hannah a signed letter on March 14 offering to sell her ''one new five-octave handbell set, with all carrying cases and accessories, for $24,600 cash on delivery not later than April 15.'' The letters crossed in the mail and were received and read by both Hannah and Daniel on March 15. In an action brought by Hannah, if Daniel denies that the parties had a binding contract on March 16, which argument most persuasively supports Hannah''s position?', 'After a few rounds of bargaining, Daniel, who operated a shop selling premium handbell sets, knew that Hannah wanted a new set for her nephew''s hymn-themed wedding procession. On March 10, Daniel sent Hannah a signed, dated note saying, ''If by Friday we land on the same handbell set and price, are we agreed?'' Hannah wrote ''Yes,'' signed below it, and returned the note on March 12. On March 14, Hannah mailed Daniel a signed letter offering to buy ''one new five-octave handbell set, with all carrying cases and accessories, for $24,600 cash on delivery not later than April 15.'' By coincidence, Daniel mailed Hannah a signed letter on March 14 offering to sell her ''one new five-octave handbell set, with all carrying cases and accessories, for $24,600 cash on delivery not later than April 15.'' The letters crossed in the mail and were received and read by both Hannah and Daniel on March 15. In an action brought