    '14438_nativity_ornament_kits', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Illusory promises',
    'For a fall Bible-story festival, Lydia, who operated a private Christian gift shop, sent Martha, an artisan who made olive-wood nativity ornament kits, a signed order for 720 kits. Martha received the order on March 3; Lydia had sent it by courier on March 1. The order required shipment no earlier than May 12 and no later than June 12. Printed in bold type across the front of the order form was this statement: “Lydia reserves the right to cancel this order at any time before May 1.” Martha’s mailed acknowledgment, stating “I accept your order,” reached Lydia on March 9. Lydia did not cancel the order. Martha shipped the kits to Lydia on May 12. The kits conformed to the order in all respects. When they arrived on June 6, Lydia refused to accept them.', 'For a fall Bible-story festival, Lydia, who operated a private Christian gift shop, sent Martha, an artisan who made olive-wood nativity ornament kits, a signed order for 720 kits. Martha received the order on March 3; Lydia had sent it by courier on March 1. The order required shipment no earlier than May 12 and no later than June 12. Printed in bold type across the front of the order form was this statement: “Lydia reserves the right to cancel this order at any time before May 1.” Martha’s mailed acknowledgment, stating “I accept your order,” reached Lydia on March 9. Lydia did not cancel the order. Martha shipped the kits to Lydia on May 12. The kits conformed to the order in all respects. When they arrived on June 6, Lydia refused to accept them.', 'Which of the following is an accurate statement as of June 6 after Lydia rejected the kits?',
