-- 14439_bookstore-launch-setup  (transformed_v1)

INSERT INTO questions (
    external_id, subject, topic, subtopic,
    fact_pattern, question_stem, call_of_question,
    difficulty, status, source_type, version, metadata
) VALUES (
    '14439_bookstore-launch-setup', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Excuse', 'Modifications',
    'Lydia owns a small private Christian bookstore. She had already purchased new display fixtures and wall lettering for a Monday morning launch of a Bible-study journal section. Lydia entered into a contract with Peter, a display installer, under which Peter was to install the fixtures and lettering for $900. The contract required Peter to do all of the work over the following weekend so the store could stay open during normal weekday business hours.

Assume the following facts. If Peter had started the installation on Saturday morning, he could have finished before Sunday evening. Instead, Peter stayed home on Saturday morning to watch the final round of a televised gospel choir competition and did not begin work until Saturday afternoon. By late Saturday afternoon, Peter realized that he had underestimated the time it would take to complete the installation over the weekend unless he hired a helper. Peter told Lydia that hiring a helper would require an additional $150 for the job. Lydia replied that she apparently had no choice and would cover whatever was needed to keep the Monday launch on schedule. Peter hired Timothy to help finish the installation and paid Timothy $150.

Lydia has offered to pay Peter $900. Peter is demanding $1,050.

**A:** $900 only, because Lydia''s promise to cover whatever was needed to keep the launch on schedule was too indefinite to be enforceable.

**B:** $1,050, because Peter''s inability to complete the installation alone made the original contract impossible to perform and Lydia''s promise created a new contract.

**C:** $900 only, because Lydia received no new consideration for her promise to pay the additional amount.

**D:** $1,050, in order to prevent Lydia''s unjust enrichment.

**Correct Answer: C**', 'Lydia owns a small private Christian bookstore. She had already purchased new display fixtures and wall lettering for a Monday morning launch of a Bible-study journal section. Lydia entered into a contract with Peter, a display installer, under which Peter was to install the fixtures and lettering for $900. The contract required Peter to do all of the work over the following weekend so the store could stay open during normal weekday business hours.

Assume the following facts. If Peter had started the installation on Saturday morning, he could have finished before Sunday evening. Instead, Peter stayed home on Saturday morning to watch the final round of a televised gospel choir competition and did not begin work until Saturday afternoon. By late Saturday afternoon, Peter realized that he had underestimated the time it would take to complete the installation over the weekend unless he hired a helper. Peter told Lydia that hiring a helper would require an additional $150 for the job. Lydia replied that she apparently had no choice and would cover whatever was needed to keep the Monday launch on schedule. Peter hired Timothy to help finish the installation and paid Timothy $150.

Lydia has offered to pay Peter $900. Peter is demanding $1,050.

**A:** $900 only, because Lydia''s promise to cover whatever was needed to keep the launch on schedule was too indefinite to be enforceable.

**B:** $1,050, because Peter''s inability to complete the installation alone made the original contract impossible to perform and Lydia''s promise created a new contract.

**C:** $900 only, because Lydia received no new consideration for her promise to pay the additional amount.

**D:** $1,050, in order to prevent Lydia''s unjust enrichment.

**Correct Answer: C**', 'How much is Peter likely to recover?',
