    'Lydia owns a small Christian craft-supply shop that sells materials to private retreat groups. On April 3, she received a signed note that Daniel had mailed the day before: "Please send me 600 blank wooden cross ornaments at your current catalog price." That afternoon, Lydia mailed Daniel a properly stamped, correctly addressed, signed letter stating, "I accept your order at my current catalog price of $4.20 per ornament." Before Daniel received Lydia''s letter, he called Lydia on April 4 and said, "I hereby revoke my order." Lydia objected. Daniel received Lydia''s letter on April 5. Because of Daniel''s call, Lydia never shipped the ornaments.', 'Lydia owns a small Christian craft-supply shop that sells materials to private retreat groups. On April 3, she received a signed note that Daniel had mailed the day before: "Please send me 600 blank wooden cross ornaments at your current catalog price." That afternoon, Lydia mailed Daniel a properly stamped, correctly addressed, signed letter stating, "I accept your order at my current catalog price of $4.20 per ornament." Before Daniel received Lydia''s letter, he called Lydia on April 4 and said, "I hereby revoke my order." Lydia objected. Daniel received Lydia''s letter on April 5. Because of Daniel''s call, Lydia never shipped the ornaments.', 'Under the relevant and prevailing rules, is there a contract between Daniel and Lydia as of April 6?',
