    'Peter contracted in a signed writing to sell to Paul a converted barn that Peter had been using as a sound-recording studio, together with the surrounding three-acre lot, for $150,000 in cash. The contract provided for exchange of the deed and purchase price on April 15. Possession was to be given to Paul on the same date.

On April 15, Peter notified Paul that because the tenant who leased the studio space wrongfully refused to quit the premises until April 30, Peter would be unable to deliver possession of the barn until then, but he assured Paul that he would tender the deed and possession on that date. Throughout April, the market value of the property was $155,000, and its fair monthly rental value was $2,000.

On April 30, Paul accepted a conveyance and possession of the barn and paid the $150,000 purchase price, but notified Peter that he was reserving any rights he might have to damages caused by Peter''s breach. Paul intended to use the studio to record a worship album and had scheduled a 15-day tracking block with musicians beginning April 15. Because he did not have possession of the barn on that date, he had to rent a mobile sound-recording trailer at a cost of $1,000 for the 15 days. Peter had no reason to know that Paul intended to use the barn as a worship-recording studio or that he had rented the mobile trailer.', 'Peter contracted in a signed writing to sell to Paul a converted barn that Peter had been using as a sound-recording studio, together with the surrounding three-acre lot, for $150,000 in cash. The contract provided for exchange of the deed and purchase price on April 15. Possession was to be given to Paul on the same date.

