    'Esther owns three small lakeside cottages that she rents to families attending private Bible retreats. In one written contract, she hired Daniel, a decorative mural artist, to paint three different Scripture-themed wall murals, one in the reading room of each cottage. The parties specified a separate price for each mural, and each mural was distinct from the other two. Daniel finished the first mural exactly as the contract required and demanded the specified price for that mural. At the same time, Daniel told Esther that he was "done with mural work" and would not even begin the other two murals. Esther refused to pay Daniel anything.', 'Esther owns three small lakeside cottages that she rents to families attending private Bible retreats. In one written contract, she hired Daniel, a decorative mural artist, to paint three different Scripture-themed wall murals, one in the reading room of each cottage. The parties specified a separate price for each mural, and each mural was distinct from the other two. Daniel finished the first mural exactly as the contract required and demanded the specified price for that mural. At the same time, Daniel told Esther that he was "done with mural work" and would not even begin the other two murals. Esther refused to pay Daniel anything.', 'Is Daniel likely to prevail in a suit for the agreed-upon price of the first mural?',
