) VALUES (
    '14549_hymn_barn_bell_lift', 'CONTRACTS', 'Third-Party Rights', 'Delegation of Duties',
    'Ruth owned a private event barn where she hosted hymn nights and small Christian retreats. Stephen, a specialist in lifting and setting heavy bell frames on high rooftops, contracted in a signed writing to lift and set a custom bronze bell frame atop Ruth''s barn. An exculpatory clause in the contract provided that Stephen would not be liable for any physical damage to Ruth''s barn occurring during installation of the bell frame. The contract also contained a per-day damages clause if Stephen did not complete performance by a specified date, and it stated that time was of the essence. Another clause provided that any later agreement for extra work under the contract had to be in writing and signed by both parties.

Well before Stephen needed to begin work, and with ample time remaining to complete performance, Stephen notified Ruth that he was selling his rigging business to Timothy, who was equally skilled in lifting and setting heavy bell frames on high rooftops. Timothy had agreed to take over the Stephen-Ruth contract. Assume that Ruth orally agreed with Stephen to accept Timothy''s services, and that Timothy performed on time but negligently installed the wrong bell frame.', 'Ruth owned a private event barn where she hosted hymn nights and small Christian retreats. Stephen, a specialist in lifting and setting heavy bell frames on high rooftops, contracted in a signed writing to lift and set a custom bronze bell frame atop Ruth''s barn. An exculpatory clause in the contract provided that Stephen would not be liable for any physical damage to Ruth''s barn occurring during installation of the bell frame. The contract also contained a per-day damages clause if Stephen did not complete performance by a specified date, and it stated that time was of the essence. Another clause provided that any later agreement for extra work under the contract had to be in writing and signed by both parties.

Well before Stephen needed to begin work, and with ample time remaining to complete performance, Stephen notified Ruth that he was selling his rigging business to Timothy, who was equally skilled in lifting and setting heavy bell frames on high rooftops. Timothy had agreed to take over the Stephen-Ruth contract. Assume that Ruth orally agreed with Stephen to accept Timothy''s services, and that Timothy performed on time but negligently installed the wrong bell frame.', 'Will Ruth succeed in an action against Stephen for damages for breach of contract?',
