    '17113_pottery-studio', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Specific performance',
    'Hannah is a master potter who commissioned Paul, a well-known ceramics designer, to design a custom kiln and glazing studio for her pottery business. Hannah told Paul she was hiring him because she trusted his unique artistic vision. Their written contract provided that ''neither party shall assign or delegate this contract without the other party''s written approval.'' After construction began, Hannah and Paul disagreed frequently about design details. When the studio was nearly three-quarters complete, Paul refused to continue working for Hannah and signed a document purporting to assign the contract to another designer. The remaining design work involved Paul''s personal creative services. Hannah immediately halted construction and sued Paul for an order directing him to specifically perform his obligations under the contract.', 'Hannah is a master potter who commissioned Paul, a well-known ceramics designer, to design a custom kiln and glazing studio for her pottery business. Hannah told Paul she was hiring him because she trusted his unique artistic vision. Their written contract provided that ''neither party shall assign or delegate this contract without the other party''s written approval.'' After construction began, Hannah and Paul disagreed frequently about design details. When the studio was nearly three-quarters complete, Paul refused to continue working for Hannah and signed a document purporting to assign the contract to another designer. The remaining design work involved Paul''s personal creative services. Hannah immediately halted construction and sued Paul for an order directing him to specifically perform his obligations under the contract.', 'Should the court grant the relief requested by Hannah?',
