    '17448_rescue-at-sea', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Promissory Estoppel',
    'After a violent hailstorm damaged the roof of a small rural church, the church''s treasurer, Martha, stood in the parking lot watching rain pour through the gaping hole into the fellowship hall below. A passing contractor, Daniel, saw the damage from the road, pulled over, and spent the entire afternoon tarping the roof and boarding up the broken rafters at his own expense, preventing further destruction to the interior. When Daniel later asked Martha to reimburse him for his labor and materials, Martha declined, saying she had never asked him to do anything.

If Daniel asserts a claim for reimbursement against Martha on a theory of promissory estoppel, which of the following would be Martha''s most effective argument in defense?

(A) The value of the church''s property is too speculative to measure.

(B) No promise of payment can be reasonably inferred from Martha''s presence at the scene.

(C) There was no consideration for any promise Martha might have made.

(D) Daniel was an officious intermeddler.

**Correct Answer: (B)**', 'After a violent hailstorm damaged the roof of a small rural church, the church''s treasurer, Martha, stood in the parking lot watching rain pour through the gaping hole into the fellowship hall below. A passing contractor, Daniel, saw the damage from the road, pulled over, and spent the entire afternoon tarping the roof and boarding up the broken rafters at his own expense, preventing further destruction to the interior. When Daniel later asked Martha to reimburse him for his labor and materials, Martha declined, saying she had never asked him to do anything.

