) VALUES (
    '18483_conference-profits', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Consequential damages',
    'Peter operates a small Christian book-publishing ministry. He contracted with Lydia, a skilled woodworker, to build custom wooden display cases for $8,000. After the contract was formed, Peter signed a separate agreement to sell books at a regional Christian conference, a venue expected to produce $30,000 in profits. Lydia failed to deliver the display cases on time, breaching the contract. Peter had not told Lydia about the conference until after Lydia''s breach. Peter now seeks the $30,000 in expected conference profits from Lydia.

**A.** Yes, because all losses that actually follow a breach are recoverable.

**B.** No, because lost profits are recoverable only in UCC goods cases.

**C.** No, because the special lost profits were not within Lydia''s reason to know at the time of contracting.

**D.** Yes, because Lydia learned of the conference profits before trial.', 'Peter operates a small Christian book-publishing ministry. He contracted with Lydia, a skilled woodworker, to build custom wooden display cases for $8,000. After the contract was formed, Peter signed a separate agreement to sell books at a regional Christian conference, a venue expected to produce $30,000 in profits. Lydia failed to deliver the display cases on time, breaching the contract. Peter had not told Lydia about the conference until after Lydia''s breach. Peter now seeks the $30,000 in expected conference profits from Lydia.

**A.** Yes, because all losses that actually follow a breach are recoverable.

**B.** No, because lost profits are recoverable only in UCC goods cases.

**C.** No, because the special lost profits were not within Lydia''s reason to know at the time of contracting.

**D.** Yes, because Lydia learned of the conference profits before trial.', 'Are those profits recoverable?',
