    '20499_kingdom_seed', 'CONTRACTS', 'Defenses to Enforceability', 'Public Policy',
    'Immediately after his graduation from Bible college in June, Stephen announced his plan to begin a one-year Church Planting Residency with his denomination''s network the following September and to marry his fiancée Tabitha in December. Stephen''s father, a longtime deacon in their church, was afraid that marriage during the demanding first year of the residency might cause Stephen to burn out or abandon the calling. He called Stephen on the phone and said that if Stephen postponed his wedding plans until after the completion of the first year of the residency, he would give him a cash "kingdom seed" of $1,000 and would pay the residency fee for the second year (the "multiplication phase"). Stephen agreed and called Tabitha to tell her that he wanted to postpone the wedding. She became so angry at him that she broke off their engagement. Two months later, Tabitha married someone else from their small group. Stephen''s father died soon after Stephen began the residency, but Stephen successfully completed his first year—his plant team even held its first preview service. Although Stephen had received strong evaluations, he decided after much prayer that he was not really called to full-time church planting and wanted instead to return to his trade as a carpenter and serve the church bi-vocationally. After deciding not to continue to the second year of the residency, he notified his father''s administrator of his decision. Stephen said that although there would be no residency fee for a second year, he expected to be paid the $1,000 cash kingdom seed that his father had promised him. The administrator refused to pay anything. If Stephen brought suit against the administrator of his father''s estate for $1,000, Stephen would probably be

A. unsuccessful, because Stephen''s father''s death terminated the offer.

B. unsuccessful, because his contract with his father violated public policy.

C. successful.

D. unsuccessful, because Stephen decided not to continue in the residency program.', 'Immediately after his graduation from Bible college in June, Stephen announced his plan to begin a one-year Church Planting Residency with his denomination''s network the following September and to marry his fiancée Tabitha in December. Stephen''s father, a longtime deacon in their church, was afraid that marriage during the demanding first year of the residency might cause Stephen to burn out or abandon the calling. He called Stephen on the phone and said that if Stephen postponed his wedding plans until after the completion of the first year of the residency, he would give him a cash "kingdom seed" of $1,000 and would pay the residency fee for the second year (the "multiplication phase"). Stephen agreed and called Tabitha to tell her that he wanted to postpone the wedding. She became so angry at him that she broke off their engagement. Two months later, Tabitha married someone else from their small group. Stephen''s father died soon after Stephen began the residency, but Stephen successfully completed his first year—his plant team even held its first preview service. Although Stephen had received strong evaluations, he decided after much prayer that he was not really called to full-time church planting and wanted instead to return to his trade as a carpenter and serve the church bi-vocationally. After deciding not to continue to the second year of the residency, he notified his father''s administrator of his decision. Stephen said that although there would be no residency fee for a second year, he expected to be paid the $1,000 cash kingdom seed that his father had promised him. The administrator refused to pay anything. If Stephen brought suit against the administrator of his father''s estate for $1,000, Stephen would probably be

