    'After Peter failed to pay a private event-supply company for the portable stage and speakers used at a ticketed Easter concert at his bookstore café, the company sued Ruth. Before the rental, Ruth had orally told the company that she would pay Peter’s $18,400 rental bill if Peter did not. That evening, Ruth signed a note to Lydia, Peter’s concert scheduler, stating: “I told Galilee Gear Rentals I would cover Peter’s $18,400 stage-and-speaker rental if Peter does not pay it.” Ruth argues that the signed note cannot satisfy the Statute of Frauds because it was sent to Lydia, not to Galilee Gear Rentals.', 'After Peter failed to pay a private event-supply company for the portable stage and speakers used at a ticketed Easter concert at his bookstore café, the company sued Ruth. Before the rental, Ruth had orally told the company that she would pay Peter’s $18,400 rental bill if Peter did not. That evening, Ruth signed a note to Lydia, Peter’s concert scheduler, stating: “I told Galilee Gear Rentals I would cover Peter’s $18,400 stage-and-speaker rental if Peter does not pay it.” Ruth argues that the signed note cannot satisfy the Statute of Frauds because it was sent to Lydia, not to Galilee Gear Rentals.', 'Can Ruth’s signed note potentially satisfy the suretyship writing requirement?',
