    '22419_parol-evidence-christian-antiquities', 'CONTRACTS', 'Contract Content and Meaning', 'Parol evidence and interpretation',
    'A Christian antiquities dealer emailed a collector offering to sell a first-century oil lamp from the Galilee region for $8,000. The collector visited the dealer''s shop, examined the lamp, and the parties signed a written contract for ''one first-century Galilee oil lamp — $8,000.'' The dealer shipped the lamp the next day. When it arrived, the collector emailed back: ''This isn''t what I ordered. I agreed to buy the first-century Judean scroll case for $8,000, not a lamp.'' The dealer insisted the written contract controlled and refused an exchange. The collector sued, alleging that before signing, the dealer had orally agreed to sell her the Judean scroll case for $8,000. The dealer denied any such oral agreement. No other witnesses were present. At trial, the collector sought to testify about the prior oral agreement. The dealer objected.', 'A Christian antiquities dealer emailed a collector offering to sell a first-century oil lamp from the Galilee region for $8,000. The collector visited the dealer''s shop, examined the lamp, and the parties signed a written contract for ''one first-century Galilee oil lamp — $8,000.'' The dealer shipped the lamp the next day. When it arrived, the collector emailed back: ''This isn''t what I ordered. I agreed to buy the first-century Judean scroll case for $8,000, not a lamp.'' The dealer insisted the written contract controlled and refused an exchange. The collector sued, alleging that before signing, the dealer had orally agreed to sell her the Judean scroll case for $8,000. The dealer denied any such oral agreement. No other witnesses were present. At trial, the collector sought to testify about the prior oral agreement. The dealer objected.', 'Should the court sustain the dealer''s objection?',
