) VALUES (
    '22422_timothy_ministry_director', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Measure of damages in major types of contract and breach',
    'Timothy was employed by Grace Community Church as its Director of Christian Education pursuant to a written contract. The contract was for a five-year term and fixed Timothy''s compensation at $2,000 per month. Timothy''s work received consistent praise from the elders. Two years after the contract began, the elders voted to reorganize the church''s discipleship ministries. As a result of the reorganization, the Director of Christian Education position was eliminated, and the church terminated Timothy''s employment. Timothy posted his resume on a prominent Christian job board and sent it to contacts in several church networks, but he did not find a job until six months after his discharge, when he began work as Associate Pastor of Discipleship at a neighboring church, performing the same general type of ministry leadership work and earning the same salary. If Timothy was successful in a suit against the church for breach of the employment contract, what damages would he be entitled to?

**A:** The full salary that Timothy would have received during the balance of the contract term.

**B:** A sum equivalent to the salary that Timothy lost between the time of his discharge and the time he began working for the new church, plus the fees he incurred posting on the Christian job board and printing resumes for his network contacts.

**C:** A transition stipend in a sum equivalent to one month''s salary.

**D:** A transition stipend in a sum equivalent to two months'' salary.', 'Timothy was employed by Grace Community Church as its Director of Christian Education pursuant to a written contract. The contract was for a five-year term and fixed Timothy''s compensation at $2,000 per month. Timothy''s work received consistent praise from the elders. Two years after the contract began, the elders voted to reorganize the church''s discipleship ministries. As a result of the reorganization, the Director of Christian Education position was eliminated, and the church terminated Timothy''s employment. Timothy posted his resume on a prominent Christian job board and sent it to contacts in several church networks, but he did not find a job until six months after his discharge, when he began work as Associate Pastor of Discipleship at a neighboring church, performing the same general type of ministry leadership work and earning the same salary. If Timothy was successful in a suit against the church for breach of the employment contract, what damages would he be entitled to?

