    'Daniel runs a Christian bookstore. In March, he sent a signed letter to Timothy, who organizes worship supplies for churches, stating: ''I will sell you 200 leather-bound hymnals at $12 each. This offer will remain open for 45 days from the date of this letter.'' Daniel signed and dated the letter on March 1. Timothy received the letter on March 3 but did not respond immediately, as he was unsure whether the upcoming Easter season would generate enough demand. Three weeks later, on March 22, Daniel received a bulk order inquiry from a large megachurch willing to purchase all the hymnals Daniel could supply at $16 per hymnal. Daniel accepted the megachurch''s offer and immediately called Timothy to revoke his March 1 offer. Timothy had been planning a major promotional push for the hymnals at his upcoming church-vendor fair but had not communicated any of this to Daniel before the call.', 'Daniel runs a Christian bookstore. In March, he sent a signed letter to Timothy, who organizes worship supplies for churches, stating: ''I will sell you 200 leather-bound hymnals at $12 each. This offer will remain open for 45 days from the date of this letter.'' Daniel signed and dated the letter on March 1. Timothy received the letter on March 3 but did not respond immediately, as he was unsure whether the upcoming Easter season would generate enough demand. Three weeks later, on March 22, Daniel received a bulk order inquiry from a large megachurch willing to purchase all the hymnals Daniel could supply at $16 per hymnal. Daniel accepted the megachurch''s offer and immediately called Timothy to revoke his March 1 offer. Timothy had been planning a major promotional push for the hymnals at his upcoming church-vendor fair but had not communicated any of this to Daniel before the call.', 'Did Daniel effectively revoke his offer?',
