    '22427_christian-musician', 'CONTRACTS', 'Third-Party Rights', 'Intended beneficiaries',
    'Peter is a worship leader at his church who also works as a luthier — he builds and repairs stringed instruments by hand. His son Timothy was about to graduate from seminary, and Peter wanted to give him a meaningful gift that reflected the craftsmanship he had poured into his life''s work. Instead of buying something, Peter decided to build a custom guitar with his own hands.

Peter entered into a written contract with Barnabas. Under the terms, Peter agreed to build a handcrafted acoustic guitar for Barnabas, doing all the work himself. In return, Barnabas agreed to pay $2,000 to Timothy on the day of Timothy''s seminary graduation, June 15, upon completion of the guitar to Barnabas''s satisfaction. Timothy did not learn of the agreement until June 15.

Before signing the written contract, Peter and Barnabas agreed orally that Barnabas would make a reasonable effort to sell his vintage amplifier to fund the payment, but that if Barnabas was unable to sell the amplifier by May 1, the agreement between them would be of no effect. Barnabas tried to sell the amplifier but found no buyer. On May 1, Barnabas told Peter that because he could not sell the amplifier, he was calling off the deal.

In an action for breach of contract brought against Barnabas by the proper party, will Barnabas be successful in asserting as a defense his inability to sell the amplifier?

**A.** Yes, because a modification of a service contract may be by oral agreement.

**B.** No, because Barnabas is estopped from denying the validity of the written agreement.

**C.** Yes, because selling the amplifier was a condition precedent to the existence of an enforceable contract.

**D.** No, because the agreement concerning the amplifier is an oral agreement that was made prior to the writing and it contradicts the terms of the writing.', 'Peter is a worship leader at his church who also works as a luthier — he builds and repairs stringed instruments by hand. His son Timothy was about to graduate from seminary, and Peter wanted to give him a meaningful gift that reflected the craftsmanship he had poured into his life''s work. Instead of buying something, Peter decided to build a custom guitar with his own hands.

Peter entered into a written contract with Barnabas. Under the terms, Peter agreed to build a handcrafted acoustic guitar for Barnabas, doing all the work himself. In return, Barnabas agreed to pay $2,000 to Timothy on the day of Timothy''s seminary graduation, June 15, upon completion of the guitar to Barnabas''s satisfaction. Timothy did not learn of the agreement until June 15.

Before signing the written contract, Peter and Barnabas agreed orally that Barnabas would make a reasonable effort to sell his vintage amplifier to fund the payment, but that if Barnabas was unable to sell the amplifier by May 1, the agreement between them would be of no effect. Barnabas tried to sell the amplifier but found no buyer. On May 1, Barnabas told Peter that because he could not sell the amplifier, he was calling off the deal.

In an action for breach of contract brought against Barnabas by the proper party, will Barnabas be successful in asserting as a defense his inability to sell the amplifier?

**A.** Yes, because a modification of a service contract may be by oral agreement.

**B.** No, because Barnabas is estopped from denying the validity of the written agreement.

**C.** Yes, because selling the amplifier was a condition precedent to the existence of an enforceable contract.

**D.** No, because the agreement concerning the amplifier is an oral agreement that was made prior to the writing and it contradicts the terms of the writing.', 'Will Barnabas be successful in asserting as a defense his inability to sell the amplifier?',
