) VALUES (
    '22470_jericho_wall_panels', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Consideration - bargain and exchange',
    'Lydia ran a private Christian theater-supply workshop. After a cancelled Bible-pageant season, eight pallets of foam-and-wood Jericho-wall panels were crowding her rented warehouse. Lydia told Peter''s set-building company that it could have the panels for free for use in its stage construction if Peter''s company brought a truck and hauled them away. Lydia made the offer because she wanted the panels gone and did not want to pay a junk hauler. Peter''s company picked up the panels. The panels turned out to be defective, and Peter''s company sued Lydia for breach of contract. Lydia argued that she had no contractual obligations because any promise she made was not supported by consideration. Lydia argued that she merely made a gift of the panels on the condition that Peter''s company pick them up.', 'Lydia ran a private Christian theater-supply workshop. After a cancelled Bible-pageant season, eight pallets of foam-and-wood Jericho-wall panels were crowding her rented warehouse. Lydia told Peter''s set-building company that it could have the panels for free for use in its stage construction if Peter''s company brought a truck and hauled them away. Lydia made the offer because she wanted the panels gone and did not want to pay a junk hauler. Peter''s company picked up the panels. The panels turned out to be defective, and Peter''s company sued Lydia for breach of contract. Lydia argued that she had no contractual obligations because any promise she made was not supported by consideration. Lydia argued that she merely made a gift of the panels on the condition that Peter''s company pick them up.', 'Can Peter''s company successfully sue for breach of contract?',
