    '22492_verse_card_sets', 'CONTRACTS', 'Formation of Contracts', 'Consideration - Illusory promises',
    'Paul operated a private Christian youth-retreat company that needed laminated verse-card sets for its summer camps. Lydia Printworks made the same verse-card sets and also sold them at a small storefront, so Lydia was worried about running short for walk-in families during a regional homeschool convention. Paul told Lydia that he understood the inventory concern. Paul then sent Lydia a signed written agreement stating that Paul would buy all of the verse-card sets his retreat company needed from Lydia Printworks, and that Lydia Printworks would sell Paul all of the verse-card sets it chose to sell. Lydia agreed, signed the writing, and returned it to Paul. A week later, Paul ordered 600 verse-card sets. Lydia refused to fill the order, saying that the homeschool convention was coming and that she wanted to keep extra sets for storefront customers. Paul sued Lydia Printworks to enforce the agreement.', 'Paul operated a private Christian youth-retreat company that needed laminated verse-card sets for its summer camps. Lydia Printworks made the same verse-card sets and also sold them at a small storefront, so Lydia was worried about running short for walk-in families during a regional homeschool convention. Paul told Lydia that he understood the inventory concern. Paul then sent Lydia a signed written agreement stating that Paul would buy all of the verse-card sets his retreat company needed from Lydia Printworks, and that Lydia Printworks would sell Paul all of the verse-card sets it chose to sell. Lydia agreed, signed the writing, and returned it to Paul. A week later, Paul ordered 600 verse-card sets. Lydia refused to fill the order, saying that the homeschool convention was coming and that she wanted to keep extra sets for storefront customers. Paul sued Lydia Printworks to enforce the agreement.', 'Should the court rule in Paul''s favor?',
