    '22501_lydia_journal_stands_notice', 'CONTRACTS', 'Remedies', 'Measure of damages in major types of contract and breach',
    'On March 2, Lydia, who operated a private Christian bookstore, ordered from Barnabas Supply 1,000 wooden journal stands for a spring Bible-study display. The order required each stand to be 12 inches wide, with delivery by March 14.

Barnabas Supply delivered the stands on March 14. Peter, Lydia''s store manager, received them and told Lydia the same day that the stands had arrived but were only 10 inches wide. Lydia intended to notify Barnabas Supply immediately, but she was preparing for a large weekend event and forgot to do so.

On April 18, Lydia received Barnabas Supply''s invoice for the stands, but she did not pay the invoice or communicate with Barnabas Supply in any way. On July 10, Barnabas Supply filed a claim against Lydia for the price of the stands. Lydia defended on the ground that the stands did not conform to the contract.', 'On March 2, Lydia, who operated a private Christian bookstore, ordered from Barnabas Supply 1,000 wooden journal stands for a spring Bible-study display. The order required each stand to be 12 inches wide, with delivery by March 14.

Barnabas Supply delivered the stands on March 14. Peter, Lydia''s store manager, received them and told Lydia the same day that the stands had arrived but were only 10 inches wide. Lydia intended to notify Barnabas Supply immediately, but she was preparing for a large weekend event and forgot to do so.

On April 18, Lydia received Barnabas Supply''s invoice for the stands, but she did not pay the invoice or communicate with Barnabas Supply in any way. On July 10, Barnabas Supply filed a claim against Lydia for the price of the stands. Lydia defended on the ground that the stands did not conform to the contract.', 'Is Barnabas Supply entitled to the price of the stands?',
