    difficulty, status, source_type, version, metadata
) VALUES (
    '22578_pool-mixup', 'CONTRACTS', 'Performance, Breach, and Discharge', 'Conditions Precedent / Failure of Condition',
    'Peter hired Timothy, a licensed contractor, to install a new stone patio and outdoor kitchen behind Peter''s home while Peter was away on a three-week mission trip to Honduras. Before leaving, Peter and Timothy signed a contract at 12% below market rate, and Peter signed a detailed work order specifying the materials, layout, and timeline. The work order listed the job site address. Timothy''s crew began work a few days later. Because the address on the work order pointed to the vacant lot next door to Peter''s house, the crew poured the foundation and installed the outdoor kitchen on the neighboring property. The neighbor was also out of the country on a church planting trip, so no one was present to redirect the crew. When Peter returned and inspected the work, he demanded that Timothy tear out the installation and rebuild it on Peter''s property at no additional cost. Timothy refused. Peter sued Timothy for breach of contract.

**A.** The contract would have resulted in a loss for Timothy.

**B.** Peter had accidentally written the wrong address in the work order.

**C.** Timothy was unsuccessful in collecting payment from the neighboring property owner.

**D.** There is another contractor who will install the patio and outdoor kitchen for Peter at the same contract price.', 'Peter hired Timothy, a licensed contractor, to install a new stone patio and outdoor kitchen behind Peter''s home while Peter was away on a three-week mission trip to Honduras. Before leaving, Peter and Timothy signed a contract at 12% below market rate, and Peter signed a detailed work order specifying the materials, layout, and timeline. The work order listed the job site address. Timothy''s crew began work a few days later. Because the address on the work order pointed to the vacant lot next door to Peter''s house, the crew poured the foundation and installed the outdoor kitchen on the neighboring property. The neighbor was also out of the country on a church planting trip, so no one was present to redirect the crew. When Peter returned and inspected the work, he demanded that Timothy tear out the installation and rebuild it on Peter''s property at no additional cost. Timothy refused. Peter sued Timothy for breach of contract.

**A.** The contract would have resulted in a loss for Timothy.

**B.** Peter had accidentally written the wrong address in the work order.

**C.** Timothy was unsuccessful in collecting payment from the neighboring property owner.

**D.** There is another contractor who will install the patio and outdoor kitchen for Peter at the same contract price.', 'Which of the following additional facts or inferences, if it was the only one true, would most likely result in a judgment for Timothy?',
