    '14563_choir_loft_emergency_sweep', 'CRIMINAL', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'Fourth Amendment searches and seizures - exceptions to warrant requirement',
    'Police received a late-night report that a loud crack and a cry for help had come from a townhouse where Mary stored supplies for a Christian youth choir. When officers arrived, they looked through a front window and saw Stephen lying on the floor beside overturned music stands. The front door was unlocked. The officers entered and found that Stephen had recently been shot and was unconscious. They called an ambulance. While waiting, one officer made a quick walk through the townhouse to see whether anyone else was inside. No one else was found, but the officer saw on the kitchen table several clear bags of what he believed to be cocaine. The officer seized the bags, and later testing confirmed that they contained cocaine. After the ambulance arrived a few minutes later and took Stephen to the hospital, the officers opened cabinets and drawers throughout the townhouse looking for the gun used in the shooting. They did not find the gun, but in a dresser drawer in an upstairs bedroom they found marijuana and seized it. Mary, who lived in the townhouse, was later charged with unlawful possession of the cocaine and the marijuana. Mary moved to suppress both items because the entry and searches were warrantless. How should the court decide the motion?', 'Police received a late-night report that a loud crack and a cry for help had come from a townhouse where Mary stored supplies for a Christian youth choir. When officers arrived, they looked through a front window and saw Stephen lying on the floor beside overturned music stands. The front door was unlocked. The officers entered and found that Stephen had recently been shot and was unconscious. They called an ambulance. While waiting, one officer made a quick walk through the townhouse to see whether anyone else was inside. No one else was found, but the officer saw on the kitchen table several clear bags of what he believed to be cocaine. The officer seized the bags, and later testing confirmed that they contained cocaine. After the ambulance arrived a few minutes later and took Stephen to the hospital, the officers opened cabinets and drawers throughout the townhouse looking for the gun used in the shooting. They did not find the gun, but in a dresser drawer in an upstairs bedroom they found marijuana and seized it. Mary, who lived in the townhouse, was later charged with unlawful possession of the cocaine and the marijuana. Mary moved to suppress both items because the entry and searches were warrantless. How should the court decide the motion?', 'How should the court decide the motion?',
