    'Two defendants, Paul and Stephen, were being tried together in federal court for robbing a federally insured bank courier who was carrying a ministry fundraiser deposit. The prosecutor sought to call a jail inmate who had worked beside Paul in the jail kitchen. The inmate would testify that Paul admitted, "Stephen and I took the courier''s bag." Paul would not testify at trial. The prosecutor asked the court to instruct the jury that the inmate''s testimony could be considered only against Paul and not against Stephen. Can the inmate''s testimony be admitted in the joint trial over Stephen''s objection?', 'Two defendants, Paul and Stephen, were being tried together in federal court for robbing a federally insured bank courier who was carrying a ministry fundraiser deposit. The prosecutor sought to call a jail inmate who had worked beside Paul in the jail kitchen. The inmate would testify that Paul admitted, "Stephen and I took the courier''s bag." Paul would not testify at trial. The prosecutor asked the court to instruct the jury that the inmate''s testimony could be considered only against Paul and not against Stephen. Can the inmate''s testimony be admitted in the joint trial over Stephen''s objection?', 'Can the inmate''s testimony be admitted in the joint trial over Stephen''s objection?',
