    'Daniel was charged with arson after a storage shed at a Christian camp burned. At a preliminary hearing, the magistrate dismissed the charge on the ground that the evidence was insufficient. The prosecutor then presented the case to a grand jury. After hearing the prosecutor''s evidence, that grand jury refused to return an indictment. A few months later, after a new grand jury had been impaneled, the prosecutor presented the same case again. The new grand jury returned an indictment charging Daniel with arson. Daniel moved to dismiss the indictment on double jeopardy grounds. Should the motion be granted?', 'Daniel was charged with arson after a storage shed at a Christian camp burned. At a preliminary hearing, the magistrate dismissed the charge on the ground that the evidence was insufficient. The prosecutor then presented the case to a grand jury. After hearing the prosecutor''s evidence, that grand jury refused to return an indictment. A few months later, after a new grand jury had been impaneled, the prosecutor presented the same case again. The new grand jury returned an indictment charging Daniel with arson. Daniel moved to dismiss the indictment on double jeopardy grounds. Should the motion be granted?', 'Should the motion be granted?',
