    '14566_choir_alibi_immunity', 'CRIMINAL', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'Fifth Amendment privilege and immunity',
    'Lydia was subpoenaed to a grand jury investigating a killing that occurred outside a winter choir concert. After giving her name and address, she was asked where she had been at the time of the killing. Lydia asked to speak with her lawyer in the hallway, was allowed to do so, and returned saying that an answer might incriminate her. The prosecutor believes Lydia''s nephew Peter committed the killing and falsely claims that he was with Lydia at the time. The prosecutor does not believe Lydia took part in the killing, but does believe her answer may expose her to other crimes.', 'Lydia was subpoenaed to a grand jury investigating a killing that occurred outside a winter choir concert. After giving her name and address, she was asked where she had been at the time of the killing. Lydia asked to speak with her lawyer in the hallway, was allowed to do so, and returned saying that an answer might incriminate her. The prosecutor believes Lydia''s nephew Peter committed the killing and falsely claims that he was with Lydia at the time. The prosecutor does not believe Lydia took part in the killing, but does believe her answer may expose her to other crimes.', 'Which step should the prosecutor take to compel Lydia to answer while causing the least harm to the prosecution''s case?',
