    'A valid warrant was issued for Ruth''s arrest. Police learned that a person with Ruth''s name and description owned a small cottage near an old hymn-singing retreat road. When officers arrived, the cottage looked empty: plywood covered the windows, the mailbox was stuffed, and weeds filled the path. A neighbor said the cottage was Ruth''s but that Ruth had not been seen there for months. Officers knocked and shouted, "Police, open up." No one answered. They pried away the plywood, broke the door, and entered. They found no person inside, but they did find an illegal short-barreled shotgun. When Ruth returned weeks later, she was charged with unlawful possession of the shotgun.', 'A valid warrant was issued for Ruth''s arrest. Police learned that a person with Ruth''s name and description owned a small cottage near an old hymn-singing retreat road. When officers arrived, the cottage looked empty: plywood covered the windows, the mailbox was stuffed, and weeds filled the path. A neighbor said the cottage was Ruth''s but that Ruth had not been seen there for months. Officers knocked and shouted, "Police, open up." No one answered. They pried away the plywood, broke the door, and entered. They found no person inside, but they did find an illegal short-barreled shotgun. When Ruth returned weeks later, she was charged with unlawful possession of the shotgun.', 'Ruth moves to suppress the shotgun. Should the court grant the motion?',
