    difficulty, status, source_type, version, metadata
) VALUES (
    '14574_jericho_wall_patrol_statement', 'CRIMINAL', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'Statements and Confessions',
    'A grand jury indicted Martha on a charge of felony vandalism after Daniel’s life-size Jericho-wall display collapsed at a privately run Bible-story festival, and a valid warrant was issued for her arrest. Officer Paul arrested Martha in a parking lot and told her that the warrant accused her of destroying the display. Paul hoped that Martha might volunteer details, but he did not give her Miranda warnings.

Paul placed Martha in the back seat of his patrol car and drove her to booking. During the drive, Martha said, “I wasn’t trying to smash Daniel’s Jericho wall. I only shoved the trumpet cart so the streamers would move, and the whole wall came down.”

At the station, after being given Miranda warnings, Martha stated that she wished to remain silent and made no other statements. Martha moved to suppress the use of her patrol-car statement as evidence on two grounds: first, that the statement was acquired without giving Miranda warnings, and second, that Officer Paul deliberately elicited her incriminating statement after she was in custody.', 'A grand jury indicted Martha on a charge of felony vandalism after Daniel’s life-size Jericho-wall display collapsed at a privately run Bible-story festival, and a valid warrant was issued for her arrest. Officer Paul arrested Martha in a parking lot and told her that the warrant accused her of destroying the display. Paul hoped that Martha might volunteer details, but he did not give her Miranda warnings.

Paul placed Martha in the back seat of his patrol car and drove her to booking. During the drive, Martha said, “I wasn’t trying to smash Daniel’s Jericho wall. I only shoved the trumpet cart so the streamers would move, and the whole wall came down.”

At the station, after being given Miranda warnings, Martha stated that she wished to remain silent and made no other statements. Martha moved to suppress the use of her patrol-car statement as evidence on two grounds: first, that the statement was acquired without giving Miranda warnings, and second, that Officer Paul deliberately elicited her incriminating statement after she was in custody.', 'As to Martha’s motion to suppress, the court should:',
