    '14580_ichthys_keyboard_case', 'CRIMINAL', 'Fourth Amendment > Arrest and Detention', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons (Criminal Procedure)',
    'Detectives obtained a valid warrant to search Peter''s rowhouse for an olive-green keyboard case with a gold fish sticker and the fentanyl tablets inside it. The warrant rested on reliable reports gathered over several weeks: Peter ran a large fentanyl-pill network, several members of the network had used violence, and firearms were usually kept in the rowhouse. That afternoon, a trusted informant reported that the keyboard case had just been delivered to Peter''s rowhouse and would be taken before sunrise to a packaging garage. Officers went to the rowhouse, knocked on the door, and called out, "Police. Open the door. We have a search warrant." After a short wait with no response, they forced the door open. Hearing scraping sounds from the basement laundry room, the officers went downstairs and found Peter gripping the olive-green keyboard case. They handcuffed Peter. A search of Peter''s person revealed a lockblade knife and a 9mm pistol. Peter shouted, "Timothy must have told you about the case!" The officers then spread through the rowhouse and looked in each room and closet. They found no other people, but one officer found a short-barreled rifle inside a toolbox on a shelf in Peter''s bedroom. In addition to charges relating to the fentanyl tablets in the keyboard case, Peter is charged with unlawful possession of weapons. Peter moves before trial to suppress the weapons seized by the officers and the statement he made.', 'Detectives obtained a valid warrant to search Peter''s rowhouse for an olive-green keyboard case with a gold fish sticker and the fentanyl tablets inside it. The warrant rested on reliable reports gathered over several weeks: Peter ran a large fentanyl-pill network, several members of the network had used violence, and firearms were usually kept in the rowhouse. That afternoon, a trusted informant reported that the keyboard case had just been delivered to Peter''s rowhouse and would be taken before sunrise to a packaging garage. Officers went to the rowhouse, knocked on the door, and called out, "Police. Open the door. We have a search warrant." After a short wait with no response, they forced the door open. Hearing scraping sounds from the basement laundry room, the officers went downstairs and found Peter gripping the olive-green keyboard case. They handcuffed Peter. A search of Peter''s person revealed a lockblade knife and a 9mm pistol. Peter shouted, "Timothy must have told you about the case!" The officers then spread through the rowhouse and looked in each room and closet. They found no other people, but one officer found a short-barreled rifle inside a toolbox on a shelf in Peter''s bedroom. In addition to charges relating to the fentanyl tablets in the keyboard case, Peter is charged with unlawful possession of weapons. Peter moves before trial to suppress the weapons seized by the officers and the statement he made.', 'As to the lockblade knife and the 9mm pistol, Peter''s motion to suppress should be',
    NULL, 'draft', 'c3_transformed', 1, '{"source_qid": "14580", "schema_variant": "transformed_v1", "outline_code": "75070100", "transformed": true, "c3_confidence": "ANCHOR_ASSISTED", "c3_verdict": "PASS"}'
