    '14582_road_to_emmaus_confession', 'CRIMINAL', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'Fifth and Sixth Amendments—Statements, Confessions, and Identifications > Right to Confrontation',
    'A private company hosted a Road to Emmaus Mystery Night in a rented warehouse. After a rival designer was found dead behind the final puzzle room, Lydia and Barnabas were charged with murder. During separate police interviews, each gave a confession that implicated both of them. Lydia later withdrew her confession and said that the detective had pressured her. Lydia and Barnabas were tried together. The prosecutor offered both confessions into evidence, and both defendants objected. After a hearing, the trial judge found that both confessions were voluntary and admitted both into evidence. Lydia testified at trial and said that her confession was false and pressured. Barnabas did not testify. Both defendants were convicted. On appeal, Lydia contends that her conviction should be reversed because Barnabas''s confession was admitted into evidence.', 'A private company hosted a Road to Emmaus Mystery Night in a rented warehouse. After a rival designer was found dead behind the final puzzle room, Lydia and Barnabas were charged with murder. During separate police interviews, each gave a confession that implicated both of them. Lydia later withdrew her confession and said that the detective had pressured her. Lydia and Barnabas were tried together. The prosecutor offered both confessions into evidence, and both defendants objected. After a hearing, the trial judge found that both confessions were voluntary and admitted both into evidence. Lydia testified at trial and said that her confession was false and pressured. Barnabas did not testify. Both defendants were convicted. On appeal, Lydia contends that her conviction should be reversed because Barnabas''s confession was admitted into evidence.', 'Lydia''s contention is',
