    'After a fatal hit-and-run outside a winter pageant rehearsal, a state grand jury learned that Stephen, the key suspect, might have kept a hand-lettered Advent gratitude ledger describing his late-night errands. Instead of asking Stephen questions, the grand jury served him with a subpoena duces tecum requiring him to produce any such ledger. The subpoena stated that the grand jury sought only the ledger itself. Stephen refused, invoking his Fifth Amendment privilege against self-incrimination.', 'After a fatal hit-and-run outside a winter pageant rehearsal, a state grand jury learned that Stephen, the key suspect, might have kept a hand-lettered Advent gratitude ledger describing his late-night errands. Instead of asking Stephen questions, the grand jury served him with a subpoena duces tecum requiring him to produce any such ledger. The subpoena stated that the grand jury sought only the ledger itself. Stephen refused, invoking his Fifth Amendment privilege against self-incrimination.', 'Under what circumstances, if any, may the grand jury compel Stephen to produce the ledger over that assertion?',
