9. Full black-letter-law verification and legal reasoning for the right answer

The credited outcome is unchanged from the source item: suppression should be denied because the aerial observation was not a Fourth Amendment search. In `California v. Ciraolo`, `476 U.S. 207` (1986), the Supreme Court held that officers did not conduct a search when they flew over a fenced backyard at 1,000 feet and saw marijuana with the naked eye, because the respondent had no reasonable expectation that the yard was protected from visual observation from public navigable airspace. In `Florida v. Riley`, `488 U.S. 445` (1989), the Court again held that naked-eye observation of curtilage from a helicopter did not violate the Fourth Amendment where the helicopter was in public airspace at an altitude sufficiently regular for public travel and the defendant showed no contrary facts.

That is the rule this item tests. Lydia clearly tried to preserve privacy with a solid wall, and the courtyard is part of the curtilage. But the Supreme Court''s aerial-surveillance cases make the controlling distinction objective reasonableness, not subjective efforts alone. On the facts given, the deputy could not see from the road, used a helicopter, saw the contraband from above, and then got a warrant. The item, like `Ciraolo` and `Riley`, assumes a lawful public-airspace vantage and a naked-eye observation no more intrusive than what a member of the public could have seen from the same location. That defeats the suppression motion. The "only purpose" point does not matter, and the "no warrant is ever required for a residential courtyard" statement is too broad. Authority: `California v. Ciraolo`, `476 U.S. 207` (1986), Cornell LII at `https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/476/207`; `Florida v. Riley`, `488 U.S. 445` (1989), Cornell LII at `https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/488/445`.

10. Brief note on why the variation preserves the original rigor/difficulty

The variation keeps the same Fourth Amendment issue, the same credited result, and the same trap structure. One distractor turns curtilage into an automatic win, one makes motive dispositive, and one overstates the result into a categorical no-warrant rule. The surface story changed from a fenced backyard marijuana patch to a walled prayer courtyard with mushroom trays, but the doctrinal task stayed identical: apply the aerial-observation cases to decide whether there was a search.

11. C3 elimination walkthrough

`CUT`

- `C` breaks first. The officer''s purpose does not change whether the defendant had an objectively reasonable expectation of privacy from the vantage point used.
- `D` breaks next. It overstates the law into a blanket rule about all residential courtyards.

`CLASH`

- `A` and `B` fight over the real axis. `A` points to curtilage and lack of a pre-viewing warrant. `B` points to the controlling question: whether aerial observation from public airspace defeats objective reasonableness.

`CALL`

- `B` wins because the aerial vantage defeats the privacy claim under the governing Fourth Amendment cases. Once there was no search at the observation stage, the warrant obtained afterward stands.

12. Divergence Audit

- Asset/subject matter changed: fenced backyard marijuana patch became a walled inner prayer courtyard with mushroom trays.
- Transaction frame changed: open-air backyard cultivation became a hidden residential courtyard cultivation setup.
- Specific facts changed: wooden fence became a stone wall, backyard became an interior courtyard, patch became trays, police officer became deputy, and county road replaced the street.
- All numbers changed: the barrier changed from 15 feet to 16 feet without crossing any doctrinal threshold.
- Fact ordering and stem rhythm changed: the residential setting, road limitation, helicopter use, and warrant sequence are presented in a new cadence.
- Cast size/roles changed: the unnamed defendant became Lydia in a hill-country cottage setting with a different law-enforcement role.
- Choice ordering changed: original key `D` moved to new key `B`.
- Choice phrasing changed: the legal substance stayed fixed, but the curtilage/courtyard and officer/deputy wording was re-skinned.
- Setting/era/texture changed: suburban backyard imagery became a hill-country cottage prayer-courtyard scene.
- Call wording preserved: "The court should" stayed because that call function is already neutral and exact.
- Recognition skip log: the aerial-view, curtilage, and warrant sequence stayed because they are load-bearing to the tested rule.

Old-stem one-sentence summary: Police received an anonymous tip that the defendant was growing marijuana in a fenced backyard, flew a helicopter over the house when the yard could not be seen from the street, saw the plants, got a warrant, and the defendant moved to suppress.

Variant-stem one-sentence summary: Deputies received an anonymous tip that Lydia was cultivating psilocybin in a walled inner prayer courtyard, flew a sheriff''s helicopter over her hill-country cottage when the courtyard could not be seen from the road, saw the trays, got a warrant, and Lydia moved to suppress.

13. Review Truth

A resident has no reasonable expectation of privacy from naked-eye aerial observation of curtilage made from public airspace at a lawful, commonly used altitude, so that observation is not a Fourth Amendment search.

