    '14609_festival_signal_stop', 'CRIMINAL', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'Fourth Amendment traffic stop and passenger exit order',
    'Officer Esther was directing traffic outside a Christian music festival at the city arena. She had only a hunch, not amounting to probable cause or reasonable suspicion, that Daniel was selling stolen Bible-study tablets. Daniel drove past in a minivan with Lydia in the passenger seat and made a right turn without signaling, in violation of the traffic code. Esther normally ignored a single missed turn signal unless it created a hazard, but she stopped Daniel because she hoped to investigate the tablet hunch.

After telling Daniel that she would issue a citation for the signal violation, Esther ordered Daniel and Lydia to step out of the minivan. When Lydia stepped out, Esther saw on the passenger seat a clear pouch of powder that she immediately recognized as cocaine. Esther arrested Daniel and Lydia for possession.

At their joint criminal trial, Daniel and Lydia argue that their Fourth Amendment rights were violated because Esther improperly (1) stopped the minivan as a pretext for investigating her hunch rather than for the stated purpose of issuing a traffic citation and (2) ordered Lydia to step out even though Esther had no reason to believe Lydia was criminal or dangerous.', 'Officer Esther was directing traffic outside a Christian music festival at the city arena. She had only a hunch, not amounting to probable cause or reasonable suspicion, that Daniel was selling stolen Bible-study tablets. Daniel drove past in a minivan with Lydia in the passenger seat and made a right turn without signaling, in violation of the traffic code. Esther normally ignored a single missed turn signal unless it created a hazard, but she stopped Daniel because she hoped to investigate the tablet hunch.

After telling Daniel that she would issue a citation for the signal violation, Esther ordered Daniel and Lydia to step out of the minivan. When Lydia stepped out, Esther saw on the passenger seat a clear pouch of powder that she immediately recognized as cocaine. Esther arrested Daniel and Lydia for possession.

At their joint criminal trial, Daniel and Lydia argue that their Fourth Amendment rights were violated because Esther improperly (1) stopped the minivan as a pretext for investigating her hunch rather than for the stated purpose of issuing a traffic citation and (2) ordered Lydia to step out even though Esther had no reason to believe Lydia was criminal or dangerous.', 'Are Daniel and Lydia correct?',
