    '14693_scripture_market_hit', 'CRIMINAL', 'Inchoate Crimes', 'Attempt',
    'Martha sold hand-lettered Scripture cards at a Saturday market. After a bitter falling-out with Lydia, who ran the booth across the aisle, Martha promised to pay Daniel $12,000 if he would kill Lydia in a way that could not be traced to Martha. Daniel bought a pistol and spent three early mornings watching the market''s rear loading alley, waiting for Lydia to arrive alone. One morning, before Daniel reached her, Lydia slipped from the loading dock behind the market, struck her head on a steel bumper, and died instantly. Believing Lydia was only unconscious, Daniel ran over and shot her twice in the chest. When Martha learned Lydia was dead, she paid Daniel the promised $12,000. A medical examiner later concluded that Lydia had already died from the fall before Daniel fired the shots.', 'Martha sold hand-lettered Scripture cards at a Saturday market. After a bitter falling-out with Lydia, who ran the booth across the aisle, Martha promised to pay Daniel $12,000 if he would kill Lydia in a way that could not be traced to Martha. Daniel bought a pistol and spent three early mornings watching the market''s rear loading alley, waiting for Lydia to arrive alone. One morning, before Daniel reached her, Lydia slipped from the loading dock behind the market, struck her head on a steel bumper, and died instantly. Believing Lydia was only unconscious, Daniel ran over and shot her twice in the chest. When Martha learned Lydia was dead, she paid Daniel the promised $12,000. A medical examiner later concluded that Lydia had already died from the fall before Daniel fired the shots.', 'The crimes below are listed in descending order of seriousness. What is the most serious crime of which Martha properly could be convicted?',
