    'Martha had just lost her catering job and needed cash before rent was due. After a church picnic, she found Ruth, an old friend from Bible study, beside the folding tables. Ruth pointed to a copper lantern sitting beside her own chair and said, “Why don’t you take that lantern? You could sell it for at least $120.” Martha said she was afraid someone would see her. Ruth promised to start loudly recounting a dramatic lost-wallet story so everyone would turn away while Martha slipped out with the lantern. Martha took the lantern and left. The lantern actually belonged to Ruth, and Ruth had been joking when she told Martha to take it.', 'Martha had just lost her catering job and needed cash before rent was due. After a church picnic, she found Ruth, an old friend from Bible study, beside the folding tables. Ruth pointed to a copper lantern sitting beside her own chair and said, “Why don’t you take that lantern? You could sell it for at least $120.” Martha said she was afraid someone would see her. Ruth promised to start loudly recounting a dramatic lost-wallet story so everyone would turn away while Martha slipped out with the lantern. Martha took the lantern and left. The lantern actually belonged to Ruth, and Ruth had been joking when she told Martha to take it.', 'Of which of the following crimes may Martha be properly convicted?',
