    'Hannah, a counselor at a Christian youth camp, borrowed $200 from the camp''s registration-fee box in the office to cover an emergency prescription for her brother. She returned the full amount on Monday morning when her paycheck was deposited. Paul, a fellow counselor, saw Hannah returning the cash and pressed her until she admitted what she had done. He stopped Hannah before she closed the office drawer and said that unless she gave him $50 from the camp''s money, he would tell the camp director. Hannah knew the director never balanced the registration box precisely and rarely noticed small discrepancies. She took $50 from the box and handed it to Paul.', 'Hannah, a counselor at a Christian youth camp, borrowed $200 from the camp''s registration-fee box in the office to cover an emergency prescription for her brother. She returned the full amount on Monday morning when her paycheck was deposited. Paul, a fellow counselor, saw Hannah returning the cash and pressed her until she admitted what she had done. He stopped Hannah before she closed the office drawer and said that unless she gave him $50 from the camp''s money, he would tell the camp director. Hannah knew the director never balanced the registration box precisely and rarely noticed small discrepancies. She took $50 from the box and handed it to Paul.', 'Of which one of the following crimes is Paul most likely to be guilty as a result of his conduct on Monday morning?',
