) VALUES (
    '16120_farmhouse-firearms-suppress', 'CRIMINAL', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons (Criminal Procedure)', 'Exclusionary Rule / Fruit of the Poisonous Tree',
    'Hannah owned a small farm with a detached workshop building that she rented to her son, Daniel, who lived and worked there as a woodworker. A deputy sheriff in the county, who was a personal friend of Hannah''s, sometimes stopped by the farm to visit. One afternoon, while Daniel was away at a lumber auction, Hannah and the deputy were having coffee on Hannah''s porch. Hannah mentioned that she was worried about Daniel because the last time she had stopped by his workshop to drop off some tools, she saw a metal case under his workbench that she suspected might contain an unregistered firearm. The deputy said, "If you want, I could take a look and let you know whether there''s anything to worry about." Hannah used her key to open the workshop door and let the deputy inside. On the workbench lay a metal case. The deputy opened it, found a sawed-off shotgun, and said, "This is definitely illegal." He took the case. Then he noticed a table saw in the corner that matched the description of a saw stolen from a nearby hardware store. Without mentioning the table saw to the Hannah, the deputy later obtained a search warrant by submitting an affidavit stating that he had personally observed items in Daniel''s workshop that he had probable cause to believe were stolen. He returned, entered, and conducted a thorough search pursuant to the warrant. The table saw turned out not to be stolen, but during the search the deputy found several power tools that had been stolen from a different hardware store. Daniel was charged with receiving stolen property. If Daniel makes a motion to suppress the stolen power tools, his motion should be

**A.** denied, since the stolen power tools were obtained as the result of a lawful search.

**B.** denied, since it would not serve the interests of justice to require a police officer to ignore a discovery that he or she has probable cause to believe is contraband.

**C.** granted, because the search warrant was issued as the result of information obtained in an unlawful search.

**D.** granted, since Daniel''s possession of stolen tools is not necessarily proof that he stole those tools.', 'Hannah owned a small farm with a detached workshop building that she rented to her son, Daniel, who lived and worked there as a woodworker. A deputy sheriff in the county, who was a personal friend of Hannah''s, sometimes stopped by the farm to visit. One afternoon, while Daniel was away at a lumber auction, Hannah and the deputy were having coffee on Hannah''s porch. Hannah mentioned that she was worried about Daniel because the last time she had stopped by his workshop to drop off some tools, she saw a metal case under his workbench that she suspected might contain an unregistered firearm. The deputy said, "If you want, I could take a look and let you know whether there''s anything to worry about." Hannah used her key to open the workshop door and let the deputy inside. On the workbench lay a metal case. The deputy opened it, found a sawed-off shotgun, and said, "This is definitely illegal." He took the case. Then he noticed a table saw in the corner that matched the description of a saw stolen from a nearby hardware store. Without mentioning the table saw to the Hannah, the deputy later obtained a search warrant by submitting an affidavit stating that he had personally observed items in Daniel''s workshop that he had probable cause to believe were stolen. He returned, entered, and conducted a thorough search pursuant to the warrant. The table saw turned out not to be stolen, but during the search the deputy found several power tools that had been stolen from a different hardware store. Daniel was charged with receiving stolen property. If Daniel makes a motion to suppress the stolen power tools, his motion should be

**A.** denied, since the stolen power tools were obtained as the result of a lawful search.

**B.** denied, since it would not serve the interests of justice to require a police officer to ignore a discovery that he or she has probable cause to believe is contraband.

**C.** granted, because the search warrant was issued as the result of information obtained in an unlawful search.

**D.** granted, since Daniel''s possession of stolen tools is not necessarily proof that he stole those tools.', 'If Daniel makes a motion to suppress the stolen power tools, his motion should be',
