    'Timothy was indicted and arrested for robbing a chapel bookstore. At his first appearance before a magistrate judge, Timothy was told of the robbery charge and told that counsel would be appointed for him the next day. After the appearance, the police required Timothy to stand with other prisoners in a lineup. Each person in the lineup had to wear a gray sweatshirt and say, "Place the offering bag on the counter." Witnesses from the bookstore identified Timothy as the robber. The next day, Timothy was arraigned on the charge.

Timothy argues that his Sixth Amendment right to counsel was violated when he was denied counsel at two critical stages: the first appearance before the magistrate judge and the lineup identification. Was Timothy''s Sixth Amendment right to counsel violated?', 'Timothy was indicted and arrested for robbing a chapel bookstore. At his first appearance before a magistrate judge, Timothy was told of the robbery charge and told that counsel would be appointed for him the next day. After the appearance, the police required Timothy to stand with other prisoners in a lineup. Each person in the lineup had to wear a gray sweatshirt and say, "Place the offering bag on the counter." Witnesses from the bookstore identified Timothy as the robber. The next day, Timothy was arraigned on the charge.

