) VALUES (
    '16173_jury-verdict-book-booth', 'CRIMINAL', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons (Criminal Procedure)', 'Right to Jury Trial',
    'At the criminal trial of Paul for armed robbery of a privately owned Christian bookstore''s pop-up booth at a Bible-quiz tournament, the prosecutor presented overwhelming evidence of guilt. Ruth, Lydia, and Barnabas, who were working at the booth, identified Paul as the robber; a latent fingerprint on the cash drawer matched Paul; and marked cash from the booth was found in Paul''s backpack. Police had arrested Paul minutes after the robbery as he rode away from the convention center on an e-bike.

When the prosecution rested, the judge directed the jury to return a verdict of guilty. Paul''s defense attorney did not object at that time. The jury withdrew, discussed the case, and agreed to a guilty verdict. The jury returned to the courtroom and announced the guilty verdict. Paul''s defense attorney then objected to the judge''s directed verdict. The court overruled the objection and sentenced Paul to 20 years in prison.', 'At the criminal trial of Paul for armed robbery of a privately owned Christian bookstore''s pop-up booth at a Bible-quiz tournament, the prosecutor presented overwhelming evidence of guilt. Ruth, Lydia, and Barnabas, who were working at the booth, identified Paul as the robber; a latent fingerprint on the cash drawer matched Paul; and marked cash from the booth was found in Paul''s backpack. Police had arrested Paul minutes after the robbery as he rode away from the convention center on an e-bike.

When the prosecution rested, the judge directed the jury to return a verdict of guilty. Paul''s defense attorney did not object at that time. The jury withdrew, discussed the case, and agreed to a guilty verdict. The jury returned to the courtroom and announced the guilty verdict. Paul''s defense attorney then objected to the judge''s directed verdict. The court overruled the objection and sentenced Paul to 20 years in prison.', 'On appeal, what should the appellate court do?',
