    '16176_daniel_bookstore_mistrial', 'CRIMINAL', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'Double Jeopardy',
    'Halfway through the first afternoon of Daniel''s criminal mischief trial, the jury had been sworn and the first witness had begun testifying about damage to a Christian bookstore''s front window. During a short sidebar in chambers about a photograph, the judge received a call that his adult son had been taken to the emergency room after a highway accident.

Without asking the prosecutor or Daniel''s lawyer whether they wanted a pause, and without considering whether the trial could simply resume the next morning, the judge returned to the courtroom, declared a mistrial, dismissed the jury, and left for the hospital. The next morning, a new jury was sworn before a different judge. Daniel objected to the second trial on double jeopardy grounds.', 'Halfway through the first afternoon of Daniel''s criminal mischief trial, the jury had been sworn and the first witness had begun testifying about damage to a Christian bookstore''s front window. During a short sidebar in chambers about a photograph, the judge received a call that his adult son had been taken to the emergency room after a highway accident.

Without asking the prosecutor or Daniel''s lawyer whether they wanted a pause, and without considering whether the trial could simply resume the next morning, the judge returned to the courtroom, declared a mistrial, dismissed the jury, and left for the hospital. The next morning, a new jury was sworn before a different judge. Daniel objected to the second trial on double jeopardy grounds.', 'Would the second trial violate the prohibition against double jeopardy?',
