    'Paul was indicted for burglary after a locked storeroom at a family-owned Christian bookstore was entered during a hymnbook inventory night. Police later arranged an in-person lineup without notifying Paul''s attorney. The only eyewitness was Lydia, a volunteer who had seen a hooded figure through a rippled glass office panel from thirty-five feet away for about two seconds before the hallway lights failed. Lydia had never met Paul before. At the lineup, Lydia identified Paul. At trial, when asked to identify the person from the storeroom, Lydia again pointed to Paul. Paul''s attorney objected and moved to suppress both identifications. What is the correct ruling on Paul''s motion?', 'Paul was indicted for burglary after a locked storeroom at a family-owned Christian bookstore was entered during a hymnbook inventory night. Police later arranged an in-person lineup without notifying Paul''s attorney. The only eyewitness was Lydia, a volunteer who had seen a hooded figure through a rippled glass office panel from thirty-five feet away for about two seconds before the hallway lights failed. Lydia had never met Paul before. At the lineup, Lydia identified Paul. At trial, when asked to identify the person from the storeroom, Lydia again pointed to Paul. Paul''s attorney objected and moved to suppress both identifications. What is the correct ruling on Paul''s motion?', 'What is the correct ruling on Paul''s motion?',
