    'Barnabas was convicted in state court of grand theft after a rare illuminated Gospel manuscript disappeared from a Christian book fair. At trial, the prosecutor introduced manuscript pages and a handwritten receipt found during a police search of a rented storage locker. Barnabas objected that the search violated the Fourth Amendment, but the trial judge admitted the evidence. The state''s highest court affirmed the conviction after rejecting the same Fourth Amendment argument. The United States Supreme Court denied Barnabas''s petition for certiorari. Barnabas then filed a petition for a writ of habeas corpus in the proper United States District Court, arguing that the evidence used to convict him was obtained in violation of the Fourth Amendment.

