    '19500_church-beam', 'CRIMINAL', 'General Principles', 'Merger Doctrine',
    'Peter was angry enough at Deacon John to want him dead. On Sunday morning, before the service, Peter crept up to the bell tower above the church entrance. A heavy oak beam — part of the bell-frame assembly — was held in place by a single rope. Peter cut the rope, expecting the beam to swing down and strike John as he walked through the doorway below. The beam swung wide, missed John completely, and shattered the church''s stained-glass window behind the doorway. The window was valued at $4,200. John was unharmed. The only damage was to the window.

The prosecutor wishes to use the transferred-intent doctrine to charge Peter with malicious destruction of the church window.

**A.** Peter is guilty of malicious destruction of property under transferred intent because he intended to cause harm and did cause harm.

**B.** Peter might face attempted murder charges for trying to kill John based on his act of cutting the rope.

**C.** Peter is not guilty of malicious destruction of property under transferred intent because the doctrine does not operate across different crime types.

**D.** Peter is not guilty of any crime because John was unharmed.', 'Peter was angry enough at Deacon John to want him dead. On Sunday morning, before the service, Peter crept up to the bell tower above the church entrance. A heavy oak beam — part of the bell-frame assembly — was held in place by a single rope. Peter cut the rope, expecting the beam to swing down and strike John as he walked through the doorway below. The beam swung wide, missed John completely, and shattered the church''s stained-glass window behind the doorway. The window was valued at $4,200. John was unharmed. The only damage was to the window.

The prosecutor wishes to use the transferred-intent doctrine to charge Peter with malicious destruction of the church window.

