    '19996_root-cellar-naomi', 'CRIMINAL', 'Other Crimes', 'False imprisonment',
    'Barnabas manages a vineyard estate and, at the end of the harvest workday, locked the underground root cellar from the outside, not realizing that Naomi — a petite worker standing five feet one inch — was still inside cataloging the autumn stores. Naomi discovered she was trapped and found that a ventilation hatch in the ceiling was unlatched. The hatch was positioned six feet above the cellar floor. Without a stepladder — and none was stored in the cellar — Naomi could not safely reach the hatch. She remained locked inside for nearly five hours until a fellow worker arrived and unlocked the door. Naomi suffered no physical injury but experienced severe distress. Barnabas contends that no false imprisonment occurred because the ceiling hatch was available as a means of exit. Under common law, did Barnabas commit false imprisonment of Naomi?', 'Barnabas manages a vineyard estate and, at the end of the harvest workday, locked the underground root cellar from the outside, not realizing that Naomi — a petite worker standing five feet one inch — was still inside cataloging the autumn stores. Naomi discovered she was trapped and found that a ventilation hatch in the ceiling was unlatched. The hatch was positioned six feet above the cellar floor. Without a stepladder — and none was stored in the cellar — Naomi could not safely reach the hatch. She remained locked inside for nearly five hours until a fellow worker arrived and unlocked the door. Naomi suffered no physical injury but experienced severe distress. Barnabas contends that no false imprisonment occurred because the ceiling hatch was available as a means of exit. Under common law, did Barnabas commit false imprisonment of Naomi?', 'Under common law, did Barnabas commit false imprisonment of Naomi?',
