    'Ruth lured nine-year-old Timothy away from a church picnic by promising him a ride to see puppies, then drove him to a remote hunting cabin she had borrowed for the weekend. Once inside the cabin, Ruth wrote out a note demanding $40,000 from Timothy''s parents and threatening that Timothy would not be returned safely unless the money was delivered within 24 hours. Before Ruth could arrange for anyone to find or receive the note, a state trooper who had been alerted by church members found the cabin and arrested Ruth. Timothy was recovered unharmed. The note was found on the cabin table, never sent.', 'Ruth lured nine-year-old Timothy away from a church picnic by promising him a ride to see puppies, then drove him to a remote hunting cabin she had borrowed for the weekend. Once inside the cabin, Ruth wrote out a note demanding $40,000 from Timothy''s parents and threatening that Timothy would not be returned safely unless the money was delivered within 24 hours. Before Ruth could arrange for anyone to find or receive the note, a state trooper who had been alerted by church members found the cabin and arrested Ruth. Timothy was recovered unharmed. The note was found on the cabin table, never sent.', 'Under common law principles, which charge best describes Ruth''s criminal liability for the taking of Timothy?',
