) VALUES (
    '20472_generator-flood-concealment', 'CRIMINAL', 'Other Crimes', 'Larceny',
    'After the Wednesday night service, Barnabas, a respected handyman and longtime member of the congregation, approached Timothy, the young deacon organizing the church''s disaster-relief supply team. Barnabas offered to sell Timothy his "like-new" portable generator for use on upcoming mission trips and storm-response calls. He pointed to the digital hour meter showing fewer than ten hours and said, "I''ve only fired it up a couple times for my own family camping trips and once for a short church workday. It''s basically brand new." The unit was clean, the housing freshly detailed, and it started on the first pull. Timothy, who needed reliable power for the relief team and his own home during outages, paid Barnabas several hundred dollars in cash — a "brother in Christ" price well below what a comparable new unit would cost — and loaded the generator into his truck. Two weeks later, while testing the unit before a predicted storm, Timothy discovered the hour meter had been tampered with and rolled back from more than 8,000 hours of prior use, the engine showed clear internal signs of having been submerged in floodwater, and the exterior had been power-washed and repainted to conceal the corrosion and heavy commercial wear.

A: Embezzlement.
B: No crime.
C: False pretenses.
D: Larceny by trick.', 'After the Wednesday night service, Barnabas, a respected handyman and longtime member of the congregation, approached Timothy, the young deacon organizing the church''s disaster-relief supply team. Barnabas offered to sell Timothy his "like-new" portable generator for use on upcoming mission trips and storm-response calls. He pointed to the digital hour meter showing fewer than ten hours and said, "I''ve only fired it up a couple times for my own family camping trips and once for a short church workday. It''s basically brand new." The unit was clean, the housing freshly detailed, and it started on the first pull. Timothy, who needed reliable power for the relief team and his own home during outages, paid Barnabas several hundred dollars in cash — a "brother in Christ" price well below what a comparable new unit would cost — and loaded the generator into his truck. Two weeks later, while testing the unit before a predicted storm, Timothy discovered the hour meter had been tampered with and rolled back from more than 8,000 hours of prior use, the engine showed clear internal signs of having been submerged in floodwater, and the exterior had been power-washed and repainted to conceal the corrosion and heavy commercial wear.

A: Embezzlement.
B: No crime.
C: False pretenses.
D: Larceny by trick.', 'What crime, if any, can Barnabas be charged with?',
    NULL, 'draft', 'c3_transformed', 1, '{"source_qid": "20472_generator-flood-concealment", "schema_variant": "transformed_v1", "outline_code": "73030100", "transformed": true, "c3_confidence": null, "c3_verdict": null}'
