    '22282_hymn_festival_permit', 'CRIMINAL', 'Inchoate Crimes', 'Conspiracy',
    'Lydia operated a booth that sold handmade Scripture journals and hymnbook covers at local Christian festivals. She wanted to set up at a city-sponsored hymn festival in the public square, but she worried that the city licensing office would not approve her vendor permit in time. A city licensing clerk told Lydia that the clerk would enter a false notation in the city''s permit database showing that Lydia''s permit had already been approved if Lydia paid the clerk an unauthorized $750 "rush fee." Lydia agreed and said she would bring the money the next morning. The next morning, Lydia arrived at the licensing office with $750, but she was told that the clerk had been fired.

A statute in the jurisdiction provides that "[a]ny person who shall give or accept a fee not authorized by law as consideration for the act of any public employee is guilty of bribery, a felony. Any person who shall offer to commit a bribery is guilty of bribery in the second degree, a felony."

