    '22303_silas_grain', 'CRIMINAL', 'Constitutional Protection of Accused Persons', 'Eighth Amendment',
    'Silas was convicted in a local jurisdiction of selling grain that was slightly short of its advertised weight, a nonviolent property offense that typically carried a minor fine or brief detention. However, because this was Silas''s seventh nonviolent offense in 15 years, he was sentenced to life in a labor quarry without the possibility of release under the state''s recidivist statute. Under the statute, Silas''s life sentence could be commuted only by a decree from the regional prefect. Silas argued that his sentence violated the constitutional prohibition against cruel and unusual punishment.

(A) Yes, because Silas had been found guilty of six previous nonviolent offenses.
(B) No, because the punishment is grossly disproportionate to the nonviolent offenses committed.
(C) Yes, because the punishment could still be commuted by a decree from the regional prefect.
(D) No, because the original offense of selling short-weighted grain only carried a minor penalty.', 'Silas was convicted in a local jurisdiction of selling grain that was slightly short of its advertised weight, a nonviolent property offense that typically carried a minor fine or brief detention. However, because this was Silas''s seventh nonviolent offense in 15 years, he was sentenced to life in a labor quarry without the possibility of release under the state''s recidivist statute. Under the statute, Silas''s life sentence could be commuted only by a decree from the regional prefect. Silas argued that his sentence violated the constitutional prohibition against cruel and unusual punishment.

