    '14775_camp_pos_records', 'EVIDENCE', 'Hearsay and Circumstances of Its Admissibility', 'Confrontation Clause; testimonial hearsay',
    'Mary is charged with robbing the cashier station at a private Christian youth conference after a county deputy stopped her behind the praise-tent parking lot and found several numbered retreat refund envelopes. Forty minutes after the robbery, the deputy assigned to the case video-recorded an interview with Daniel, an attendee who had seen the robber; Daniel described the holdup and the robber’s clothing.

The deputy then arranged a lineup, at which Ruth, the registration cashier who was robbed, identified Mary as the robber. The deputy later obtained the conference vendor’s electronic point-of-sale records for that day’s meal-card reloads and refund payouts, which allowed the state to calculate the amount of cash missing from the cashier station.

Three weeks later, Ruth testified before a grand jury, which indicted Mary. Ruth and Daniel both died of unrelated causes before trial.

At Mary’s trial, which of the following evidence, if properly authenticated, may be admitted against Mary over her attorney’s objection that its receipt would violate the confrontation right?

