    '14844_divorce-to-deacon', 'EVIDENCE', 'Presentation of Evidence', 'Truthfulness',
    'A church hired a licensed contractor to build a new fellowship hall. After the work was completed, the church sued the contractor for breach of contract, claiming the building had serious structural defects. The church called a structural engineer as an expert witness to testify about the cost of repairs. On cross-examination, the contractor''s attorney asked the engineer whether he had previously provided false testimony as a witness in his own divorce proceedings. The engineer denied it. The contractor''s attorney then seeks to introduce a certified transcript from the divorce proceeding showing that the engineer had indeed testified falsely under oath. The transcript is

A: inadmissible, because impeachment on a collateral issue may not be proved by extrinsic evidence.
B: admissible, because the questioning occurred on cross-examination of the expert witness.
C: inadmissible, because a witness''s character for truthfulness cannot be attacked by specific instances of conduct.
D: admissible, because the transcript is self-authenticating documentary evidence.

