    '18709_lydia_bookshop_delivery_statement', 'EVIDENCE', 'Presentation of Evidence', 'Prior inconsistent statement',
    'In a federal negligence trial over a delivery-van collision outside a private Christian bookshop, Lydia calls Stephen, expecting him to testify that the delivery van rolled through a stop sign before striking a handbell cart. Stephen instead testifies that the van stopped before entering the intersection. While Stephen is still on the stand, Lydia shows him a signed but unsworn statement he gave to an investigator two days after the collision, in which he said the van did not stop. Stephen denies making the statement. Lydia offers the statement only to impeach Stephen. The defendant objects that Lydia called Stephen as her own witness.', 'In a federal negligence trial over a delivery-van collision outside a private Christian bookshop, Lydia calls Stephen, expecting him to testify that the delivery van rolled through a stop sign before striking a handbell cart. Stephen instead testifies that the van stopped before entering the intersection. While Stephen is still on the stand, Lydia shows him a signed but unsworn statement he gave to an investigator two days after the collision, in which he said the van did not stop. Stephen denies making the statement. Lydia offers the statement only to impeach Stephen. The defendant objects that Lydia called Stephen as her own witness.', 'How should the court rule?',
