) VALUES (
    '19094_olive_wood_crucifix', 'EVIDENCE', 'Relevancy and Excluding Relevant Evidence', 'Habit or custom practice',
    '> A Customs and Border Protection officer, Hannah, had been told by a confidential informant that a passenger arriving from Tel Aviv on a particular El Al flight would be carrying heroin concealed inside a hollowed religious souvenir. Officer Hannah went to Newark Liberty International Airport and waited at the international arrivals hall with a drug-detection dog specially trained to recognize the odor of narcotics. When the defendant, Daniel, walked past carrying a small zippered shoulder pouch, the dog sat down and began pawing the air in front of him. Officer Hannah stopped Daniel and searched the pouch. Inside she found a small olive-wood crucifix pendant — the kind sold to pilgrims in the Old City of Jerusalem — with a hollowed cross chamber at its base. When the chamber was opened, it held a single foil-wrapped brick of heroin weighing roughly one ounce. Daniel, who was arrested and charged with the illegal importation of a controlled substance, told the agent he had bought the pendant two days earlier as a personal devotional item and had no idea anything was hidden inside. At Daniel''s federal trial, the prosecution offers to prove that Daniel was convicted twelve years earlier of importing heroin by hiding it in the hollowed base of a small olive-wood crucifix pendant. If Daniel''s attorney objects, the court should rule that evidence of Daniel''s prior conviction is

**(A)** admissible, because it is evidence of a distinctive method of operation.
**(B)** inadmissible, because the prior conviction occurred more than 10 years before the trial.
**(C)** inadmissible, because evidence of previous conduct by a defendant may not be used against him or her.
**(D)** admissible, as evidence of habit.', '> A Customs and Border Protection officer, Hannah, had been told by a confidential informant that a passenger arriving from Tel Aviv on a particular El Al flight would be carrying heroin concealed inside a hollowed religious souvenir. Officer Hannah went to Newark Liberty International Airport and waited at the international arrivals hall with a drug-detection dog specially trained to recognize the odor of narcotics. When the defendant, Daniel, walked past carrying a small zippered shoulder pouch, the dog sat down and began pawing the air in front of him. Officer Hannah stopped Daniel and searched the pouch. Inside she found a small olive-wood crucifix pendant — the kind sold to pilgrims in the Old City of Jerusalem — with a hollowed cross chamber at its base. When the chamber was opened, it held a single foil-wrapped brick of heroin weighing roughly one ounce. Daniel, who was arrested and charged with the illegal importation of a controlled substance, told the agent he had bought the pendant two days earlier as a personal devotional item and had no idea anything was hidden inside. At Daniel''s federal trial, the prosecution offers to prove that Daniel was convicted twelve years earlier of importing heroin by hiding it in the hollowed base of a small olive-wood crucifix pendant. If Daniel''s attorney objects, the court should rule that evidence of Daniel''s prior conviction is

**(A)** admissible, because it is evidence of a distinctive method of operation.
**(B)** inadmissible, because the prior conviction occurred more than 10 years before the trial.
**(C)** inadmissible, because evidence of previous conduct by a defendant may not be used against him or her.
**(D)** admissible, as evidence of habit.', 'If Daniel''s attorney objects, the court should rule that evidence of Daniel''s prior conviction is',
    NULL, 'draft', 'c3_transformed', 1, '{"source_qid": "19094_olive_wood_crucifix", "schema_variant": "transformed_v1", "outline_code": "32020500", "transformed": true, "c3_confidence": "ANCHOR_ASSISTED", "c3_verdict": "PASS"}'
