    '22143_gospel_drama_recording', 'EVIDENCE', 'Hearsay and Circumstances of Its Admissibility', 'Confrontation Clause',
    'After a gospel-drama rehearsal at a rented community theater, Timothy struck Barnabas with a wooden shepherd''s staff. Timothy claimed that he was protecting his sister Naomi, who had just told him that Barnabas had cornered her near the prop room. At Timothy''s assault trial, the prosecutor played a video-recorded interview that Naomi gave to police later that night because Naomi was too frightened to appear in court. In the interview, Naomi described what happened before the blow and repeated several statements Timothy had made. Her account contradicted Timothy''s claim that he acted to protect her. Timothy had never had any opportunity to cross-examine Naomi about the recorded interview. The jury convicted Timothy of assault. On appeal, Timothy argues that playing the interview violated his constitutional rights.', 'After a gospel-drama rehearsal at a rented community theater, Timothy struck Barnabas with a wooden shepherd''s staff. Timothy claimed that he was protecting his sister Naomi, who had just told him that Barnabas had cornered her near the prop room. At Timothy''s assault trial, the prosecutor played a video-recorded interview that Naomi gave to police later that night because Naomi was too frightened to appear in court. In the interview, Naomi described what happened before the blow and repeated several statements Timothy had made. Her account contradicted Timothy''s claim that he acted to protect her. Timothy had never had any opportunity to cross-examine Naomi about the recorded interview. The jury convicted Timothy of assault. On appeal, Timothy argues that playing the interview violated his constitutional rights.', 'Were Timothy''s constitutional rights violated?',
