    '22180_pottery-kiln-hannah', 'EVIDENCE', 'Presentation of Evidence', 'Objections and Motions to Strike',
    'Hannah was injured when the unguarded door of a pottery kiln at Stephen''s artisan studio swung open and scalded her forearm during a public demonstration. She commenced an action for damages three years and eight months later, just before the applicable four-year limitations period expired. In his opening statement, Stephen''s attorney told the jury that the lengthy delay in filing suit showed that Hannah had not suffered any real injury and that her claimed damages were exaggerated. Hannah testified at trial. During cross-examination, Stephen''s attorney asked her when she first retained legal counsel in connection with the incident. Hannah answered, "Not until about two months ago — the studio manager came to me shortly after the accident and offered to settle for $5,000, and I was trying to negotiate a higher amount." Stephen''s attorney moved to strike the portion of the answer referencing the settlement discussions. Should the motion to strike be granted?', 'Hannah was injured when the unguarded door of a pottery kiln at Stephen''s artisan studio swung open and scalded her forearm during a public demonstration. She commenced an action for damages three years and eight months later, just before the applicable four-year limitations period expired. In his opening statement, Stephen''s attorney told the jury that the lengthy delay in filing suit showed that Hannah had not suffered any real injury and that her claimed damages were exaggerated. Hannah testified at trial. During cross-examination, Stephen''s attorney asked her when she first retained legal counsel in connection with the incident. Hannah answered, "Not until about two months ago — the studio manager came to me shortly after the accident and offered to settle for $5,000, and I was trying to negotiate a higher amount." Stephen''s attorney moved to strike the portion of the answer referencing the settlement discussions. Should the motion to strike be granted?', 'Should the motion to strike be grante