) VALUES (
    '22227_barn-fire-daniel', 'EVIDENCE', 'Presentation of Evidence', 'Refreshing recollection',
    'Three years passed before a negligence suit against a landowner reached trial. The plaintiff alleged that the defendant''s poorly maintained fence allowed livestock to escape onto a neighboring road, causing a collision that injured the plaintiff. At trial, the plaintiff''s attorney called Daniel, an eyewitness to the accident. Daniel testified that before coming to court, he had refreshed his recollection by reviewing written notes he had taken during his conversation with the plaintiff''s attorney that occurred two weeks after the accident. On proper motion by the defendant''s attorney, what should the court do?

A: Admit the notes into evidence as an admission of a party.
B: Strike Daniel''s testimony, unless it is shown that the notes themselves are unavailable.
C: Direct that the notes be brought into court for inspection by the defendant''s attorney.
D: Direct that the notes cannot be brought into court because they have not been offered into evidence.', 'Three years passed before a negligence suit against a landowner reached trial. The plaintiff alleged that the defendant''s poorly maintained fence allowed livestock to escape onto a neighboring road, causing a collision that injured the plaintiff. At trial, the plaintiff''s attorney called Daniel, an eyewitness to the accident. Daniel testified that before coming to court, he had refreshed his recollection by reviewing written notes he had taken during his conversation with the plaintiff''s attorney that occurred two weeks after the accident. On proper motion by the defendant''s attorney, what should the court do?

A: Admit the notes into evidence as an admission of a party.
