    difficulty, status, source_type, version, metadata
) VALUES (
    '22228_construction-timothy', 'EVIDENCE', 'Presentation of Evidence', 'Witnesses',
    'During the plaintiff''s case-in-chief in a negligence action, the plaintiff''s attorney called Timothy, a civil engineer specializing in construction-site safety, to testify about structural failures in temporary scaffolding. When the plaintiff''s attorney began to question Timothy about his professional credentials, the defendant''s attorney stated on the record, in the presence of the jury, that she would stipulate that Timothy was qualified as an expert in construction safety, and she objected to any further questions on his qualifications. The court found that Timothy was qualified as an expert in construction safety. Should the plaintiff''s attorney be permitted to continue questioning Timothy about his qualifications?

A: No, because the court must independently verify a witness''s expertise and cannot allow the parties to stipulate to qualifications.
B: No, because the qualifications of the expert are no longer in issue.
C: Yes, because the jury may consider an expert''s qualifications in determining credibility.
D: Yes, because the court''s finding that the witness is qualified does not bar further inquiry into the details of his expertise.', 'During the plaintiff''s case-in-chief in a negligence action, the plaintiff''s attorney called Timothy, a civil engineer specializing in construction-site safety, to testify about structural failures in temporary scaffolding. When the plaintiff''s attorney began to question Timothy about his professional credentials, the defendant''s attorney stated on the record, in the presence of the jury, that she would stipulate that Timothy was qualified as an expert in construction safety, and she objected to any further questions on his qualifications. The court found that Timothy was qualified as an expert in construction safety. Should the plaintiff''s attorney be permitted to continue questioning Timothy about his qualifications?

A: No, because the court must independently verify a witness''s expertise and cannot allow the parties to stipulate to qualifications.
B: No, because the qualifications of the expert are no longer in issue.
C: Yes, because the jury may consider an expert''s qualifications in determining credibility.
D: Yes, because the court''s finding that the witness is qualified does not bar further inquiry into the details of his expertise.', 'Should the plaintiff''s attorney be permitted to continue questioning Timothy about his qualifications?',
