    'Naomi was pulling a folding orchard wagon manufactured by Lydia''s company when the wagon crossed a shallow driveway lip and Naomi lost control. After the incident, the wagon''s tow bar was found severely bent. Naomi claimed the tow bar bent during ordinary pulling and caused the loss of control, so she sued Lydia''s company for personal injuries on the ground that the tow bar was defectively designed and not strong enough for normal use. Lydia''s company denied any defect and claimed Naomi yanked the wagon into a stone planter and that the tow bar did not bend until that impact. During trial, the judge gave the jury a special instruction about the uncertainty of negative evidence. Lydia''s company then called Timothy, its vice president in charge of customer safety. Timothy testified that he had held that job for seven years, that the company had sold more than 12,000 identical wagons during that time, and that he had never heard of a tow bar bending during ordinary pulling. If Naomi''s attorney objects, should Timothy''s testimony be admitted?', 'Naomi was pulling a folding orchard wagon manufactured by Lydia''s company when the wagon crossed a shallow driveway lip and Naomi lost control. After the incident, the wagon''s tow bar was found severely bent. Naomi claimed the tow bar bent during ordinary pulling and caused the loss of control, so she sued Lydia''s company for personal injuries on the ground that the tow bar was defectively designed and not strong enough for normal use. Lydia''s company denied any defect and claimed Naomi yanked the wagon into a stone planter and that the tow bar did not bend until that impact. During trial, the judge gave the jury a special instruction about the uncertainty of negative evidence. Lydia''s company then called Timothy, its vice president in charge of customer safety. Timothy testified that he had held that job for seven years, that the company had sold more than 12,000 identical wagons during that time, and that he h