    '22239_kiln-explosion', 'EVIDENCE', 'Privileges and Other Policy Exclusions', 'Attorney-client',
    'John owns a company that manufactures ceramic kilns for pottery studios. After a kiln exploded during normal use, killing the studio owner, the state charged John''s company with criminal negligence. At the criminal trial, a ceramics safety expert testified under oath for the prosecution that in his opinion the kiln''s temperature-control system was defectively designed, and that any time the kiln was operated above 1,800°F, a catastrophic seal failure was inevitable. Subsequently, Mary was severely burned when a kiln manufactured by John''s company exploded while she was firing pottery in a neighboring state. Mary brought a personal injury action against the company in that state. Pursuant to a subpoena served on the company, Mary''s attorney called upon the company to produce records of thermal-stress tests the company had performed on the kiln before selling it. The company''s attorney objected, on the ground that the company had turned the test records over to its attorney in preparation for the criminal trial. Should the trial court require production of the records?', 'John owns a company that manufactures ceramic kilns for pottery studios. After a kiln exploded during normal use, killing the studio owner, the state charged John''s company with criminal negligence. At the criminal trial, a ceramics safety expert testified under oath for the prosecution that in his opinion the kiln''s temperature-control system was defectively designed, and that any time the kiln was operated above 1,800°F, a catastrophic seal failure was inevitable. Subsequently, Mary was severely burned when a kiln manufactured by John''s company exploded while she was firing pottery in a neighboring state. Mary brought a personal injury action against the company in that state. Pursuant to a subpoena served on the company, Mary''s attorney called upon the company to produce records of thermal-stress tests the company had performed on the kiln before selling it. The company''s attorney o