) VALUES (
    '14906_barnabas-v-lydia-land-deal', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Contracts for Land', 'Time for performance',
    'Barnabas owned a small vineyard that he had been cultivating for years. Six months before Lydia''s college graduation, Barnabas orally promised Lydia that he would give her the vineyard as a gift on her graduation day. Lydia, anticipating the gift, entered into a valid written contract to sell the vineyard to Martha, with the closing to take place "one week after Lydia''s college graduation." Barnabas failed to convey the vineyard to Lydia on her graduation day. On the intended closing date — one week after graduation — Martha first learned that Lydia could not convey the vineyard because Barnabas had broken his promise. Martha considered suing Lydia but realized she could not compel conveyance while Barnabas still held title. Two weeks after graduation, Barnabas died. Under his valid will, Barnabas devised the vineyard to Lydia. Within a week, the executor of Barnabas''s estate delivered to Lydia an executor''s deed to the vineyard in compliance with state law. Martha promptly learned of this transfer and demanded that Lydia convey the vineyard to her. Lydia refused. Martha sued Lydia for specific performance. Who will likely prevail?', 'Barnabas owned a small vineyard that he had been cultivating for years. Six months before Lydia''s college graduation, Barnabas orally promised Lydia that he would give her the vineyard as a gift on her graduation day. Lydia, anticipating the gift, entered into a valid written contract to sell the vineyard to Martha, with the closing to take place "one week after Lydia''s college graduation." Barnabas failed to convey the vineyard to Lydia on her graduation day. On the intended closing date — one week after graduation — Martha first learned that Lydia could not convey the vineyard because Barnabas had broken his promise. Martha considered suing Lydia but realized she could not compel conveyance while Barnabas still held title. Two weeks after graduation, Barnabas died. Under his valid will, Barnabas devised the vineyard to Lydia. Within a week, the executor of Barnabas''s estate delivered to Lydia an executor''s deed to the vineyard in compliance with state law. Martha promptly learned of this transfer and demanded that Lydia convey the vineyard to her. Lydia refused. Martha sued Lydia for specific performance. Who will likely prevail?', 'Who will likely prevail?',
