) VALUES (
    '14911_lakecabin_wildfire', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Contracts for Land', 'Risk of Loss and Equitable Conversion',
    'A retired schoolteacher, Mary, owned a small lakefront cabin on a half-acre lot she had used for years as a summer getaway. Six months ago Mary moved into an assisted-living apartment, and the cabin has been vacant ever since. Daniel and Ruth, a young married couple, gave Mary a signed handwritten offer to purchase the cabin. The offer was unconditional and sufficient to satisfy the statute of frauds, and when Mary signed an acceptance, an enforceable contract resulted. Three weeks after the parties had entered into their contract, two weeks after Daniel and Ruth had obtained a written mortgage lending commitment from a lender, and one week before the agreed-upon closing date, a wildfire swept through the area and burned the cabin to the ground. The loss was not insured, because two years earlier Mary had let her homeowner''s insurance policy lapse after she had paid off the mortgage on the cabin and stopped using it as a regular residence. The handwritten contract was wholly silent as to matters of financing, risk of loss, and insurance. Daniel and Ruth declared the contract voided by the fire, but Mary asserted a right to enforce the contract despite the loss. There is no applicable statute. If a court finds for Mary, what will be the likely reason?', 'A retired schoolteacher, Mary, owned a small lakefront cabin on a half-acre lot she had used for years as a summer getaway. Six months ago Mary moved into an assisted-living apartment, and the cabin has been vacant ever since. Daniel and Ruth, a young married couple, gave Mary a signed handwritten offer to purchase the cabin. The offer was unconditional and sufficient to satisfy the statute of frauds, and when Mary signed an acceptance, an enforceable contract resulted. Three weeks after the parties had entered into their contract, two weeks after Daniel and Ruth had obtained a written mortgage lending commitment from a lender, and one week before the agreed-upon closing date, a wildfire swept through the area 