    'Esther owned a corner parcel in a downtown arts district, improved with an old brick building used as a private Christian bookstore and journal-printing studio called Olive Branch Editions. The building filled the lot on three sides. On the alley side, a roll-up freight door opened into a short sunken delivery bay. When delivery vans backed down the bay and workers unloaded paper pallets, the rear half of each van sat in the public alley and narrowed the fire-access lane. As vans became longer, the obstruction became more serious. The city advised Esther in writing that the delivery bay could no longer be used because vans blocked the fire-access alley; it gave Esther 60 days to cease and desist. During the 60 days, Esther sold and conveyed the parcel by general warranty deed for substantial consideration to Timothy. The delivery-bay notice was not posted at the building and was not discussed during negotiations. When the 60 days expired, the city required Timothy to stop using the delivery bay. This action substantially reduced the value of the parcel. Timothy brought an appropriate action against Esther seeking cancellation of the deed and return of all monies paid. Such action should be based upon a claim of', 'Esther owned a corner parcel in a downtown arts district, improved with an old brick building used as a private Christian bookstore and journal-printing studio called Olive Branch Editions. The building filled the lot on three sides. On the alley side, a roll-up freight door opened into a short sunken delivery bay. When delivery vans backed down the bay and workers unloaded paper pallets, the rear half of each van sat in the public alley and narrowed the fire-access lane. As vans became longer, the obstruction became more serious. The city advised Esther in writing that the delivery bay could no longer be used because vans blocked the fire-access alley; it gave Esther 60 days to cease and desist. During the 60 days, Esther sold and conveyed the parcel