    'Four months ago, Daniel signed a written contract to buy Lydia''s small orchard cottage, Selah House, for $132,000. Daniel paid Lydia a $6,000 deposit to be credited against the purchase price at closing. The contract stated that, if Daniel defaulted, Lydia could elect to keep the deposit as liquidated damages. Closing was scheduled for two weeks ago. Seven weeks before the closing date, Daniel''s employer reassigned him to a job 850 miles away. Daniel immediately told Lydia that he would not close and demanded the return of the $6,000 deposit. Lydia refused. She waited until the scheduled closing date had passed, listed Selah House through a broker, and then conveyed it to Mary for $129,000. Mary paid the full purchase price and promptly recorded her deed. Mary knew about Daniel''s earlier contract with Lydia. In an appropriate action, Daniel seeks to recover the $6,000 deposit from Lydia. The most probable result will be that Lydia', 'Four months ago, Daniel signed a written contract to buy Lydia''s small orchard cottage, Selah House, for $132,000. Daniel paid Lydia a $6,000 deposit to be credited against the purchase price at closing. The contract stated that, if Daniel defaulted, Lydia could elect to keep the deposit as liquidated damages. Closing was scheduled for two weeks ago. Seven weeks before the closing date, Daniel''s employer reassigned him to a job 850 miles away. Daniel immediately told Lydia that he would not close and demanded the return of the $6,000 deposit. Lydia refused. She waited until the scheduled closing date had passed, listed Selah House through a broker, and then conveyed it to Mary for $129,000. Mary paid the full purchase price and promptly recorded her deed. Mary knew about Daniel''s earlier contract with Lydia. In an appropriate action, Daniel seeks to recover the $6,000 deposit from Lydia. The most probable result will be that Lydia', 'In an appropriate action, Daniel seeks to recover the $6,000 deposit from Lydia. The most pro