) VALUES (
    '14920_farmstead', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Contracts for Land', 'Marketable Title',
    'Twenty years ago, a farmer executed a warranty deed conveying a 40-acre tract to a Bible college. The granting clause read: "to the Bible college and its successors forever, so long as the premises are used for religious instruction." The Bible college built a campus on the land and operated there for two decades. The college later decided to relocate to a larger facility and entered into a valid written contract to sell the land to a developer for $2 million. The developer intended to build a commercial warehouse complex on the site. The contract contained no provision addressing the quality of title the college was required to convey. When the developer learned of the language in the original deed, it refused to close. The college sued the developer for specific performance and joined the farmer as a party. Is the college likely to prevail?

**(A)** No, because the quoted provision is an enforceable restrictive covenant that runs with the land.

**(B)** No, because the farmer''s possibility of reverter makes the college''s title unmarketable.

**(C)** Yes, because the farmer''s warranty deed conveyed a fee simple absolute and the farmer cannot derogate from his own grant.

**(D)** Yes, because a charitable trust to support religious education will attach to the sale proceeds.

**Correct Answer: B** (Original Key: A)', 'Twenty years ago, a farmer executed a warranty deed conveying a 40-acre tract to a Bible college. The granting clause read: "to the Bible college and its successors forever, so long as the premises are used for religious instruction." The Bible college built a campus on the land and operated there for two decades. The college later decided to relocate to a larger facility and entered into a valid written contract to sell the land to a developer for $2 million. The developer intended to build a commercial warehouse complex on the site. The contract contained no provision addressing the quality of title the college was required to convey. When the developer learned of the language in the original deed, it refused to close. The college sued the developer for specific performance and joined the farmer as a party. Is the college likely to prevail?

