    '14932_cedarbrook_scripture_studio', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Contracts for Land', 'Essential terms',
    'Martha owned Cedarbrook Cottage in fee simple. Four years ago, Martha and Timothy agreed that Timothy could occupy the cottage month to month while he ran a private Scripture-card studio there, with Timothy paying Martha rent each month. After eight months of Timothy''s occupancy, Martha told Timothy that he could buy Cedarbrook Cottage if the monthly burden stayed close to what he had been paying as rent. Martha and Timothy orally agreed that Timothy would pay $18,000 in cash, the annual property taxes, the annual hazard-insurance premiums, the costs of maintaining Cedarbrook Cottage, and the monthly mortgage payments that Martha owed on the property. They also orally agreed that within five years Timothy could pay whatever mortgage balance remained due and Martha would give him a warranty deed to Cedarbrook Cottage.

Timothy''s average monthly payments did turn out to be almost the same as his former monthly rent. Timothy fully complied with every obligation he had undertaken. He also made structural changes to Cedarbrook Cottage, including reinforcing the rear room and adding a permanent accessible entry for the studio. Cedarbrook Cottage is now worth 45% more than it was when Martha and Timothy made their oral agreement. Timothy arranged financing and was ready to complete the purchase, but Martha refused to close. Timothy brought an appropriate action for specific performance against Martha to enforce the agreement. The court should rule for', 'Martha owned Cedarbrook Cottage in fee simple. Four years ago, Martha and Timothy agreed that Timothy could occupy the cottage month to month while he ran a private Scripture-card studio there, with Timothy paying Martha rent each month. After eight months of Timothy''s occupancy, Martha told Timothy that he could buy Cedarbrook Cottage if the monthly burden stayed close to what he had been paying as rent. Martha and Timothy orally agreed that Timothy would pay $18,000 in cash, the annual property taxes, the annual hazard-insurance premiums, the costs of maintaining Cedarbrook Cottage, and the monthly mortgage payments that Martha owed on the property. They also orally agreed that within five years Timothy could pay whatever mortgage balance remained due and Martha would give him a warranty deed to Cedarbrook Cottage.

