    'Hannah owns a six-acre vineyard that she purchased several years ago with her own savings. More recently, she borrowed $200,000 from the Antioch Community Bank and secured the loan with a mortgage on the entire vineyard. A year after that, she borrowed an additional $75,000 from Lydia, a private lender, and gave Lydia a second mortgage on the same six-acre vineyard. Both mortgages were promptly recorded. Hannah has since defaulted on both loans. Antioch Bank initiated foreclosure proceedings and sent proper notice to all necessary parties. The vineyard''s current fair market value is $400,000. Lydia filed a timely motion in the foreclosure proceeding asking the court to compel Antioch Bank to foreclose first on only three of the six acres, leaving the other three acres available for Lydia''s security. Antioch Bank opposes the motion and insists it may subject the entire six-acre vineyard to the foreclosure sale. Will the court grant Lydia''s motion?

