    'Peter''s estate plan included a revocable trust established 38 years ago, with a trust company as trustee. The principal asset of the trust has always been Hopeacre, a profitable, debt-free olive grove and farmstand property. The trust instrument instructed the trustee to pay the net income to Peter for life; after Peter''s death, to pay the net income to Peter''s wife, Hannah, for life; and after Hannah''s death, to distribute the net trust estate as Hannah may appoint by will, or, if she failed to exercise this power of appointment, to Peter''s son John, who was Hannah''s stepson.

Peter died 31 years ago survived by Hannah and John. Peter had not revoked or amended the trust agreement. Six years after Peter''s death, Hannah married Timothy. She then had a daughter, Ruth, was widowed a second time, and died last year. Hannah''s will contained only one dispositive provision: "I give my entire estate to my daughter, Ruth, and I intentionally make no provision for my stepson, John." Ruth is now 24 years old.

