    '14985_hillside-vineyard-wall', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Ownership', 'Rights and duties of co-tenants',
    'Peter and Barnabas, two friends, owned a terraced hillside vineyard in fee simple as tenants in common. Together they signed a mortgage on the vineyard to a local credit union. Peter built a stone wall along what he believed was the true boundary between the vineyard and the adjoining orchard owned by Stephen. Soon afterward Peter quarreled with Barnabas and walked away from the vineyard, leaving Barnabas in sole possession. The debt secured by the mortgage has not been paid. Stephen later surveyed his orchard and discovered that the wall Peter had built a year earlier did not follow the true line: part of the wall stood inside the vineyard, and part of it encroached on Stephen''s orchard. Stephen and Barnabas executed an agreement fixing the boundary at the line of Peter''s wall. The agreement met every formality the jurisdiction required and was promptly and properly recorded. A year after the agreement was recorded, Peter patched up his quarrel with Barnabas and resumed joint possession of the vineyard. Peter then repudiated the boundary agreement and brought an appropriate action against Stephen and Barnabas to quiet title along the original true boundary.

