) VALUES (
    '14990_silent-festival-parcels', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Ownership', 'Waste',
    'Barnabas owned in fee simple two fenced parcels beside a county fairground. The parcels were empty, generated no rent, and had never been used for parking, farming, storage, or any other income-producing purpose. Barnabas kept them only because he hoped a planned highway exit would someday make them valuable.

Barnabas died. By his duly probated will, he left the residue of his estate, including the two parcels, to his sister Martha for life, with remainder in fee simple to his niece Ruth. The executor distributed the estate under an appropriate court order and told Martha that future real estate taxes on the parcels were her responsibility. Except for statutes relating to probate and real estate taxes, there is no applicable statute.

Martha did not pay the real estate taxes due on the parcels. To prevent a tax sale of the fee simple, Ruth paid the taxes and demanded reimbursement from Martha. Martha refused, and Ruth brought an appropriate action against Martha to recover the amount paid.

**A.** the present value, measured over Martha''s expected lifetime, of the investment return Ruth lost by paying the tax bill now.

**B.** nothing, because Martha''s present possession gave her the right to decide whether taxes on the empty parcels should be paid.

**C.** nothing, because Martha received no rents, profits, or other income from the parcels.

**D.** the amount paid, because a life tenant must pay current charges on property held for life.', 'Barnabas owned in fee simple two fenced parcels beside a county fairground. The parcels were empty, generated no rent, and had never been used for parking, farming, storage, or any other income-producing purpose. Barnabas kept them only because he hoped a planned highway exit would someday make them valuable.

