    'For an injury arising from the condition or use of a common element in a common-interest community, the association is the proper liability target. Individual unit owners are not personally liable solely because they are association members, share rights in the common area, or were present. A board member is not personally liable merely because he voted for ordinary association action, absent independent tortious conduct, bad faith, willful misconduct, or action outside authority.

**Pass-1 Step 2 - invariant layer preserved.**

The legal issue remains personal liability for an association common-area tort. The credited outcome remains that the individual unit owner is not liable because the association is the only liable party on these facts. The dominant trap remains the answer that correctly spares the unit owner but adds personal liability for the board member because he voted for the opening. The array geometry remains two "No" answers and two "Yes" answers, with the final clash between association-only liability and association-plus-board-member liability.

**Pass-1 Step 5 - locked final Christian variation.**

A private townhome development has a Scripture garden, stone path, and covered hymn pavilion that serve as a shared common area for owners and residents. Title to the garden and pavilion is held by the development''s homeowners'' association. Although the development has been open for several months, many homes remain unsold. With Resurrection Sunday open-house tours approaching, the association''s board decided to unlock the garden earlier than scheduled, hoping that the amenity would help attract new buyers.

On the first afternoon the garden was unlocked, only a small group of residents was present: Stephen, a board member, and his wife Lydia; Ruth, a unit owner who came by herself; and Hannah, a unit owner with her two children. While the children followed the engraved verses along the stone path, the adults discussed the board''s decision to unlock the garden early. Stephen stated that he had voted in favor of the early opening.

As one child stepped away from the path beside the pavilion, he slipped on loose gravel and fell. Hannah brought a lawsuit naming three defendants: the homeowners'' association, Stephen personally, and Ruth personally. All owners are also members of the association. Assuming the court finds that a tort occurred, is Ruth personally liable?', 'For an injury arising from the condition or use of a common element in a common-interest community, the association is the proper liability target. Individual unit owners are not personally liable solely because they are association members, share rights in the common area, or were present. A board member is not personally liable merely because he voted for ordinary association action, absent independent tortious conduct, bad faith, willful misconduct, or action outside authority.

