    difficulty, status, source_type, version, metadata
) VALUES (
    '15026_solar-barn', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Rights in Land', 'Non-Possessory Rights and Interests in Land',
    'Peter and Daniel own neighboring parcels in a small town. Each parcel has a single-story barn used to store farm equipment. The two barns are the same height. Daniel decided to tear down his barn and build a much taller equipment storage facility, as permitted by all applicable zoning and building ordinances. He secured every required governmental permit before breaking ground. While Daniel''s new structure was going up, Peter realized that the deep shadow it would cast would leave his barn in near-permanent shade during the morning hours, making the interior so dim that he would have to install electric lighting to use the space — a cost he had not anticipated when he built the barn. Peter brought an appropriate action against Daniel to enjoin construction and for damages. Peter presented uncontroverted evidence that the shadow would materially increase his operating costs. No statute, ordinance, covenant, or easement (other than the building and zoning ordinances) applies to the issues before the court. The court should

**A.** award Peter damages measured by the increased operating costs, but not an injunction.

**B.** grant Peter the requested injunction.

**C.** grant judgment for Daniel, because Peter has no legal right to have sunlight continue to reach his barn.

**D.** grant judgment for Daniel, because he had secured all necessary governmental approvals for the new structure.

**Correct answer: C**', 'Peter and Daniel own neighboring parcels in a small town. Each parcel has a single-story barn used to store farm equipment. The two barns are the same height. Daniel decided to tear down his barn and build a much taller equipment storage facility, as permitted by all applicable zoning and building ordinances. He secured every required governmental permit before breaking ground. While Daniel''s new structure was going up, Peter realized that the deep shadow it would cast would leave his barn in near-permanent shade during the morning hours, making the interior so dim that he would have to install electric lighting to use the space — a cost he had not anticipated when he built the barn. Peter brought an appropriate action against Daniel to enjoin construction and for damages. Peter presented uncontroverted evidence that the shadow would materially increase his operating costs. No statute, ordinance, covenant, or easement (other than the building and zoning ordinances) applies to the issues before the court. The court should

**A.** award Peter damages measured by the increased operating costs, but not an injunction.

**B.** grant Peter the requested injunction.

**C.** grant judgment for Daniel, because Peter has no legal right to have sunlight continue to reach his barn.

