    '15039_psalm_studio_judgment', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Titles', 'Recording Acts',
    'The land records for a narrow city lot called the Harp Room still listed Martha as owner in fee simple. Martha had signed a contract to sell the lot, where she operated a private psalm-calligraphy studio, to Timothy. Twelve days after the contract, Timothy paid the agreed price and received Martha''s warranty deed. Neither the contract nor the deed was recorded. Five days after that closing, while Martha still kept her hymn-lettering presses in the studio, Daniel, a creditor, properly filed a money judgment against Martha. Daniel had no knowledge of Timothy''s interest. A judgment statute creates a lien on real property then owned or later acquired by the judgment debtor. The recording act protects subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the conveyance or mortgage is recorded. Daniel sued to enforce the lien against the parcel in Timothy''s hands.', 'The land records for a narrow city lot called the Harp Room still listed Martha as owner in fee simple. Martha had signed a contract to sell the lot, where she operated a private psalm-calligraphy studio, to Timothy. Twelve days after the contract, Timothy paid the agreed price and received Martha''s warranty deed. Neither the contract nor the deed was recorded. Five days after that closing, while Martha still kept her hymn-lettering presses in the studio, Daniel, a creditor, properly filed a money judgment against Martha. Daniel had no knowledge of Timothy''s interest. A judgment statute creates a lien on real property then owned or later acquired by the judgment debtor. The recording act protects subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the conveyance or mortgage is recorded. Daniel sued to enforce the lien against the parcel in Timothy''s hands.', 'If the court rules for Timothy, it most probably will be because:',
