    'Samuel owned a small citrus orchard parcel on the edge of town. After years of hosting private Bible-study picnics there, Samuel signed an instrument in the proper form of a deed, purporting to convey the orchard parcel to his longtime friend Ruth. Samuel handed the instrument to Ruth and said, "The orchard is yours now, but please keep it out of the county records until after my memorial service. If my family sees it before then, they will pressure me about it every week."

Three days later, Samuel asked Ruth to give the deed back because he had decided that it would be simpler to leave the orchard to her by will. Ruth said she would burn the deed. A few days later, Ruth falsely told Samuel that she had burned it. Samuel never made a will. Six months ago, Samuel died intestate, survived by his son Timothy as his only heir. The morning after Samuel''s burial, Ruth recorded the deed. When Timothy learned of the recording and Ruth''s claim to the orchard, he brought an appropriate action against Ruth to quiet title.', 'Samuel owned a small citrus orchard parcel on the edge of town. After years of hosting private Bible-study picnics there, Samuel signed an instrument in the proper form of a deed, purporting to convey the orchard parcel to his longtime friend Ruth. Samuel handed the instrument to Ruth and said, "The orchard is yours now, but please keep it out of the county records until after my memorial service. If my family sees it before then, they will pressure me about it every week."

