    'After Lydia was hospitalized with a severe infection, she properly executed a deed sufficient to convey her small lakeside cottage to her nephew Daniel, who was away in another state with a Christian disaster-relief crew. Two people signed as witnesses so the deed would qualify for recording under an applicable statute. The law of the jurisdiction requires only two witnesses for a will to be properly executed.

Lydia handed the deed to Stephen, Daniel''s close friend, and said, "I want Daniel to have the cottage. Please take this deed for him." A few days later, Stephen learned that Lydia was unlikely to survive. The day before Lydia died, Stephen recorded the deed.

Daniel returned shortly after Lydia''s death, learned about the deed, and took possession of the cottage. Lydia died intestate, leaving her daughter Ruth as her sole heir. When Ruth asserted ownership of the cottage, Daniel brought an appropriate action against Ruth to determine title. If the court rules for Daniel and against Ruth, what will be the most likely explanation?', 'After Lydia was hospitalized with a severe infection, she properly executed a deed sufficient to convey her small lakeside cottage to her nephew Daniel, who was away in another state with a Christian disaster-relief crew. Two people signed as witnesses so the deed would qualify for recording under an applicable statute. The law of the jurisdiction requires only two witnesses for a will to be properly executed.

