    '15049_emmaus_recording_loft', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Titles', 'Conveyance by Operation of Law and Will',
    'Matthew died with a valid will that was admitted to probate. His estate included a converted downtown recording loft where he had hosted private hymn-writing nights, along with substantial personal property. In the will, Matthew specifically devised the loft to Lydia, a longtime friend identified by name. The rest of Matthew''s estate was given to the Emmaus Literacy Mission, a stated charity.

Lydia was alive when Matthew signed the will, but she died before Matthew. Lydia''s husband, Paul, and Lydia''s daughter Ruth, who has a disability, both survived Matthew. After Matthew signed the will, the city rezoned the block for residential studios, and the loft rose sharply in value. Credible extrinsic evidence shows that Matthew wanted Lydia to have the loft so that Lydia and Paul could care for Ruth there. No applicable statute addresses the situation.', 'Matthew died with a valid will that was admitted to probate. His estate included a converted downtown recording loft where he had hosted private hymn-writing nights, along with substantial personal property. In the will, Matthew specifically devised the loft to Lydia, a longtime friend identified by name. The rest of Matthew''s estate was given to the Emmaus Literacy Mission, a stated charity.

