    difficulty, status, source_type, version, metadata
) VALUES (
    '15050_bfp-judgment-lien-vineyard', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Titles', 'Bona fide purchaser',
    'Naomi owned a vineyard in fee simple, as the county land records showed. Naomi entered into a valid written contract to convey the vineyard to Peter. At closing, Peter paid the purchase price in full and received a general warranty deed in proper form, signed by Naomi, purporting to convey the vineyard to Peter. Peter did not then record the deed or take possession of the vineyard.

Next, a creditor (who had no knowledge of the contract or the deed) obtained a substantial money judgment against Naomi. Then, Peter recorded the deed from Naomi. Thereafter, the creditor properly filed the judgment against Naomi.

A statute of the jurisdiction provides: "Any judgment properly filed shall, for 10 years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered."

Afterward, Peter entered into a written contract to convey the vineyard to Lydia. Lydia objected to Peter''s title and refused to close. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: "Unless the same be recorded according to law, no conveyance shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice."

Peter brought an appropriate action to require Lydia to complete the purchase contract. The court should decide for

**A:** Lydia, because the creditor''s judgment was obtained before Peter recorded the deed from Naomi.

**B:** Lydia, because even though Naomi''s deed to Peter prevented the creditor''s judgment from being a lien on the vineyard, the creditor''s filed judgment poses a threat of litigation.

**C:** Peter, because he received the deed from Naomi before the creditor filed the judgment.

**D:** Peter, because he recorded his deed before the creditor filed the judgment.', 'Naomi owned a vineyard in fee simple, as the county land records showed. Naomi entered into a valid written contract to convey the vineyard to Peter. At closing, Peter paid the purchase price in full and received a general warranty deed in proper form, signed by Naomi, purporting to convey the vineyard to Peter. Peter did not then record the deed or take possession of the vineyard.

Next, a creditor (who had no knowledge of the contract or the deed) obtained a substantial money judgment against Naomi. Then, Peter recorded the deed from Naomi. Thereafter, the creditor properly filed the judgment against Naomi.

A statute of the jurisdiction provides: "Any judgment properly filed shall, for 10 years from filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered."

Afterward, Peter entered into a written contract to convey the vineyard to Lydia. Lydia objected to Peter''s title and refused to close. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: "Unless the same be recorded according to law, no conveyance shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice."

Peter brought an appropriate action to require Lydia to complete the purchase contract. The court should decide for

**A:** Lydia, because the creditor''s judgment was obtained before Peter recorded the deed from Naomi.

**B:** Lydia, because even though Naomi''s deed to Peter prevented the creditor''s judgment from being a lien on the vineyard, the creditor''s filed judgment poses a threat of litigation.

**C:** Peter, because he received the deed from Naomi before the creditor filed the judgment.

**D:** Peter, because he recorded his deed before the creditor filed the judgment.', 'The court should decide for',
