    'A faithful widow named Lydia owned a small olive-pressing business and taught practical skills and Scripture to young women in the village fellowship. An elder in the church named Barnabas owned a hillside olive orchard in fee simple. Barnabas conveyed the orchard to Lydia by warranty deed for value, intending that she expand her pressing work and host teaching gatherings under the trees for the fellowship. Shortly after the conveyance, a man from the coastal city asserted that his grandfather had retained an interest in the orchard through an old family arrangement and brought an appropriate action against Lydia to quiet title to the entire orchard. Lydia demanded that Barnabas defend her title under the deed''s covenant of warranty, but Barnabas refused. Lydia then successfully defended the action at her own expense, proving through land records that Barnabas''s title was clear and the city man''s claim had no legal basis. Lydia brought an appropriate action against Barnabas to recover the expenses she incurred in defending against the quiet title action. In this action, the court should decide for

