    'Esther owned Olive House, a small Scripture-retreat cottage on a wooded tract. Lydia owned Psalm Meadow, an adjoining undeveloped tract suitable for a home. Lydia executed and delivered a deed granting Esther a right-of-way over the eastern 20 feet of Psalm Meadow for convenient ingress and egress to a county road, although Esther''s tract also touched a different county road. Esther did not record the deed. Esther then graded the described 20-foot strip, laid a gravel lane, installed a simple wooden gate at the lane entrance, and began using the lane openly and visibly. Later, Lydia borrowed $18,000 from a bank and gave the bank a mortgage on Psalm Meadow. The bank promptly and properly recorded the mortgage. Esther then recorded Lydia''s deed granting the right-of-way. Lydia later defaulted on the loan. The jurisdiction''s recording act provides: "No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law." In a proper foreclosure action concerning Psalm Meadow, brought against Esther and Lydia, the bank seeks to have Esther''s right-of-way declared subordinate to the bank''s mortgage so the right-of-way will be terminated by completion of the foreclosure.', 'Esther owned Olive House, a small Scripture-retreat cottage on a wooded tract. Lydia owned Psalm Meadow, an adjoining undeveloped tract suitable for a home. Lydia executed and delivered a deed granting Esther a right-of-way over the eastern 20 feet of Psalm Meadow for convenient ingress and egress to a county road, although Esther''s tract also touched a different county road. Esther did not record the deed. Esther then graded the described 20-foot strip, laid a gravel lane, installed a simple wooden gate at the lane entrance, and began using the lane openly and visibly. Later, Lydia borrowed $18,000 from a bank and gave the bank a mortgage on Psalm Meadow. The bank promptly and properly recorded the mor