    '15065_manna-meal-bakers-deed', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Requirements for valid deed', 'Titles',
    'Lydia owned Cedar Lantern Orchard, a valuable tract of land in Jordan County. Wanting the local Bible-meal volunteers to use the land for shared fellowship work, Lydia executed a document in the form of a warranty deed. The deed was regular in every respect except that the only words identifying the grantees in both the granting clause and the habendum clause were: "the bread bakers for every Wednesday-night Bible meal in Jordan County."

The instrument was acknowledged as required by statute and promptly and properly recorded. Lydia told her lawyer Paul, but no one else, that she used that wording because she hoped those bakers would sell the orchard fruit and fund countywide fellowship meals. Nine days later, Lydia died suddenly and unexpectedly, leaving a will that devised her entire estate to Ruth.

After probate of the will became final and the administration of Lydia''s estate was closed, Ruth brought an appropriate action to quiet title to Cedar Lantern Orchard and properly served as defendants each Wednesday-night Bible-meal group located in the county. The only evidence introduced consisted of Lydia''s chain of title, the probated will, the recorded deed, the fact that no one except Lydia and Paul knew about the deed, and Lydia''s conversation with Paul.', 'Lydia owned Cedar Lantern Orchard, a valuable tract of land in Jordan County. Wanting the local Bible-meal volunteers to use the land for shared fellowship work, Lydia executed a document in the form of a warranty deed. The deed was regular in every respect except that the only words identifying the grantees in both the granting clause and the habendum clause were: "the bread bakers for every Wednesday-night Bible meal in Jordan County."

The instrument was acknowledged as required by statute and promptly and properly recorded. Lydia told her lawyer Paul, but no one else, that she used that wording because she hoped those bakers would sell the orchard fruit and fund countywide fellowship meals. Nine days later, Lydia died suddenly and unexpectedly, leaving a will that devised her entire estate to Ruth.

After probate of the will became final and the administration of Lydia''s estate was closed, Ruth brought an appropriate action to quiet title to Cedar Lantern Orchard and properly served as defendants each Wednesday-night Bible-meal group located in the county. The only evidence introduced consisted of Lydia''s chain of title, the probated will, the recorded deed, the fact that no one except Lydia and Paul knew about the deed, and Lydia''s conversation with Paul.', 'In the action, judgment should be for',
