    '15081_cedar_ridge_apiary_notice', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Titles', 'Types of notice',
    'Six years ago, Naomi, before moving to another state, conveyed Cedar Ridge, a secluded tract with a small cabin and spring, to Timothy by quitclaim deed. Timothy paid no consideration. Timothy, who was 21 years old and had little business experience, moved onto Cedar Ridge, kept beehives there, maintained the cabin, and sold honey from the tract, but he neglected to record his deed.

Later, Naomi conveyed Cedar Ridge to Barnabas by warranty deed. Barnabas, a commercial stone-and-land promoter, paid valuable consideration for the deed to him. Barnabas was unaware of Timothy''s possession, Timothy''s quitclaim deed, or Timothy''s relationship to Naomi. Barnabas promptly and properly recorded his deed and began removing sandstone from the tract.

As soon as Timothy learned of Barnabas''s work, Timothy recorded his deed and brought an appropriate action to enjoin Barnabas from removing the stone and to quiet title. The recording act of the jurisdiction provides: "No conveyance or mortgage of real property shall be good against subsequent purchasers for value and without notice unless the same be recorded according to law." In this action, Barnabas should', 'Six years ago, Naomi, before moving to another state, conveyed Cedar Ridge, a secluded tract with a small cabin and spring, to Timothy by quitclaim deed. Timothy paid no consideration. Timothy, who was 21 years old and had little business experience, moved onto Cedar Ridge, kept beehives there, maintained the cabin, and sold honey from the tract, but he neglected to record his deed.

Later, Naomi conveyed Cedar Ridge to Barnabas by warranty deed. Barnabas, a commercial stone-and-land promoter, paid valuable consideration for the deed to him. Barnabas was unaware of Timothy''s possession, Timothy''s quitclaim deed, or Timothy''s relationship to Naomi. Barnabas promptly and properly recorded his deed and began removing sandstone from the tract.

