    '17809_olive_ink_conduit', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Titles, Deeds, and Conveyancing', 'Titles',
    'Several years ago, Olive & Ink Press, a Bible-bookbinding company, planned to run a buried fiber-optic conduit from its print shop to a Scripture-gift storage barn several miles away. To do that, the company negotiated for rights to place and maintain the conduit across several privately owned parcels. In return for a cash payment, Martha, then the owner of a lavender farm, executed a right-of-way deed allowing Olive & Ink Press to install and maintain the underground conduit across the farm. The right-of-way deed was properly recorded.

The farm later passed through several recorded intermediate conveyances. None of those deeds mentioned the right of way. About 15 years after the right-of-way deed was recorded, Peter acquired the farm. Two years later, Peter agreed in a written contract to sell the farm to Ruth, and the contract required Peter to furnish Ruth with an abstract of title. Peter hired Cedar Abstract, a reputable abstract company, to prepare the abstract. Cedar Abstract prepared the abstract and delivered it to Peter, but the abstract omitted any mention of Olive & Ink Press''s recorded right-of-way deed. Peter delivered the abstract to Ruth.

After reviewing the abstract, Ruth paid the full purchase price. Peter conveyed the farm to Ruth by a deed containing covenants of general warranty and against encumbrances. At the closing, Peter, Ruth, and Cedar Abstract were all unaware of Olive & Ink Press''s right-of-way deed. Nearly a year later, Olive & Ink Press notified Ruth that it planned to begin installing the underground conduit on its right of way across the farm. Ruth then asserted a claim against Cedar Abstract for damages caused by the existence of the right of way.', 'Several years ago, Olive & Ink Press, a Bible-bookbinding company, planned to run a buried fiber-optic conduit from its print shop to a Scripture-gift storage barn several miles away. To do that, the company negotiated for rights to place and maintain the conduit across several privately owned parcels. In return for a cash payment, Martha, then the owner of a lavender farm, executed a right-of-way deed allowing Olive & Ink Press to install and maintain the underground conduit across the farm. The right-of-way deed was properly recorded.

The farm later passed through several recorded intermediate conveyances. None of those deeds mentioned the right of way. About 15 years after the right-of-way deed was recorded, Peter acquired the farm. Two years later, Peter agreed in a written contract to sell the farm to Ruth, and the contract required Peter to furnish Ruth with an abstract of title. Peter hired Cedar Abstract, a reputable abstract company, to prepare the abstract. Cedar Abstract prepared the abstract and delivered it to Peter, but the abstract omitted any mention of Olive & Ink Press''s recorded right-of-way deed. Peter delivered the abstract to Ruth.

After reviewing the abstract, Ruth paid the full purchase price. Peter conveyed the farm to Ruth by a deed containing covenants of general warranty and against encumbrances. At the closing, Peter, Ruth, and Cedar Abstract were all unaware of Olive & Ink Press''s right-of-way deed. Nearly a year later, Olive & Ink Press notified Ruth that it planned to begin installing the underground conduit on its right of way across the farm. Ruth then asserted a claim against Cedar Abstract for damages caused by the existence of the right of way.', 'The court should find for',
