) VALUES (
    '17971_good_shepherd_apiary_deed', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Titles', 'Types of deeds',
    'When Daniel''s aunt died, she left him a 300-acre parcel of realty known locally as the Good Shepherd Apiary. Soon afterward, Daniel executed a general warranty deed to his sister Ruth, conveying realty described as "the east 30 acres of the parcel of land known as the Good Shepherd Apiary." Daniel later executed a quitclaim deed in favor of Lydia to "all my right, title, and interest in a parcel of realty known as the Good Shepherd Apiary."

The Good Shepherd Apiary was a famous piece of land in the area and was easily identifiable by its name. However, the jurisdiction was one in which reference to government survey markers was customarily used in describing realty in general warranty deeds.

A. No, because Daniel''s deed to Ruth described the property conveyed with reasonable clarity.

B. Yes, because a deed that conveys only a portion of a parcel must describe that portion by metes and bounds or other survey terminology.

C. Yes, because the jurisdiction is one in which reference to government survey markers is customarily used in describing realty in general warranty deeds.

D. No, because one who executes a quitclaim deed does not warrant that he or she holds any interest in the property described.', 'When Daniel''s aunt died, she left him a 300-acre parcel of realty known locally as the Good Shepherd Apiary. Soon afterward, Daniel executed a general warranty deed to his sister Ruth, conveying realty described as "the east 30 acres of the parcel of land known as the Good Shepherd Apiary." Daniel later executed a quitclaim deed in favor of Lydia to "all my right, title, and interest in a parcel of realty known as the Good Shepherd Apiary."

The Good Shepherd Apiary was a famous piece of land in the area and was easily identifiable by its name. However, the jurisdiction was one in which reference to government survey markers was customarily used in describing realty in general warranty deeds.

A. No, because Daniel''s deed to Ruth described the property conveyed with reasonable clarity.

B. Yes, because a deed that conveys only a portion of a parcel must describe that portion by metes and bounds or other survey terminology.

C. Yes, because the jurisdiction is one in which reference to government survey markers is customarily used in describing realty in general warranty deeds.

D. No, because one who executes a quitclaim deed does not warrant that he or she holds any interest in the property described.', 'If Lydia subsequently institutes a proceeding against Ruth seeking to declare herself the owner of the entire Good Shepherd Apiary, should the court grant the relief requested by Lydia?',
