    'After negotiation, Ruth and Daniel entered into a valid written contract for the sale to Daniel of Ruth''s cottage and adjoining garden lot. The contract required closing of title "on or before September 30" because Daniel was moving his family into town that evening and needed to move into the cottage immediately. The contract also stated that "time is of the essence." On September 28, Ruth told Daniel that she would not be able to close until October 1. On October 1, Ruth tendered a deed. Although Ruth complied with the requirements of the contract in all other respects, Daniel refused to accept the deed on the ground that the date for performance had passed.', 'After negotiation, Ruth and Daniel entered into a valid written contract for the sale to Daniel of Ruth''s cottage and adjoining garden lot. The contract required closing of title "on or before September 30" because Daniel was moving his family into town that evening and needed to move into the cottage immediately. The contract also stated that "time is of the essence." On September 28, Ruth told Daniel that she would not be able to close until October 1. On October 1, Ruth tendered a deed. Although Ruth complied with the requirements of the contract in all other respects, Daniel refused to accept the deed on the ground that the date for performance had passed.', 'If Ruth asserts a claim against Daniel as a result of Daniel''s refusal to accept Ruth''s tender on October 1, the court should find for',
