    'Naomi owned, in fee simple absolute, a lakeside cabin lot. In a valid will, she devised the lot to "those of my nieces who survive me, in equal shares, as joint tenants and not as tenants in common, with full right of survivorship." When Naomi signed the will, Ruth and Esther, both adults, were her only nieces. Two months later, Ruth borrowed $18,000 from Cedar Gate Credit Union and signed a note secured by a mortgage on the cabin lot. A few weeks later, Ruth died without repaying the loan. The next spring, Naomi died, survived by Esther as her only surviving niece. A state statute provides that a mortgagee of realty holds equitable title to the realty.', 'Naomi owned, in fee simple absolute, a lakeside cabin lot. In a valid will, she devised the lot to "those of my nieces who survive me, in equal shares, as joint tenants and not as tenants in common, with full right of survivorship." When Naomi signed the will, Ruth and Esther, both adults, were her only nieces. Two months later, Ruth borrowed $18,000 from Cedar Gate Credit Union and signed a note secured by a mortgage on the cabin lot. A few weeks later, Ruth died without repaying the loan. The next spring, Naomi died, survived by Esther as her only surviving niece. A state statute provides that a mortgagee of realty holds equitable title to the realty.', 'Which statement best describes Esther''s interest in the cabin lot after Naomi''s death?',
