    '19498_bell-meadow-retreat', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Titles, Deeds, and Conveyancing > Conveyance by Deed > After-acquired title', 'Titles',
    'Mary owned a privately held hill-country retreat property used for small Christian study weekends. Her title papers made it appear that the property included an 18-acre tract called Mercy Field and an adjoining 4-acre tract called Bell Meadow.

On March 3, Mary executed a general warranty deed containing covenants of title and quiet enjoyment. The deed purported to convey to Stephen "a 22-acre retreat property consisting of an 18-acre tract known as Mercy Field and a 4-acre tract known as Bell Meadow."

On May 12, Stephen sold the same described property to Hannah, executing a general warranty deed that described both Mercy Field and Bell Meadow and contained covenants of title.

On June 6, Mary learned that before her own title papers were prepared, the prior owner had already sold Bell Meadow to Paul. Mary, unaware of Stephen''s conveyance to Hannah, offered Paul $12,000 to convey Bell Meadow to Stephen. On June 8, Paul accepted Mary''s offer and conveyed Bell Meadow to Stephen by general warranty deed containing covenants of title.

Hannah institutes an appropriate proceeding for an order declaring herself to be the owner of Bell Meadow.

Which of the following would be Hannah''s most effective argument in support of her claim?', 'Mary owned a privately held hill-country retreat property used for small Christian study weekends. Her title papers made it appear that the property included an 18-acre tract called Mercy Field and an adjoining 4-acre tract called Bell Meadow.

