    '22002_kiln_room', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Ownership', 'Nature and Ownership of Land',
    'Barnabas owned a row of craftsmen''s workshops and leased one of them to Lydia, a potter. Their signed lease covered the entire unit — both the front throwing room, where Lydia shaped her vessels, and the back kiln room, where she fired them. For several months Lydia worked the whole shop and paid her rent on time. Then Barnabas, who kept his own shop next door, grew irritated that smoke from Lydia''s kiln drifted through his window. He bought a padlock, carried Lydia''s drying racks out of the kiln room, locked its door, and told her she was no longer allowed inside it. Lydia said Barnabas was being unreasonable, but she kept making and selling pottery from the front throwing room. She did, however, stop paying any rent until Barnabas "came to his senses." Barnabas sued Lydia for the unpaid rent. The jurisdiction followed the majority rule.', 'Barnabas owned a row of craftsmen''s workshops and leased one of them to Lydia, a potter. Their signed lease covered the entire unit — both the front throwing room, where Lydia shaped her vessels, and the back kiln room, where she fired them. For several months Lydia worked the whole shop and paid her rent on time. Then Barnabas, who kept his own shop next door, grew irritated that smoke from Lydia''s kiln drifted through his window. He bought a padlock, carried Lydia''s drying racks out of the kiln room, locked its door, and told her she was no longer allowed inside it. Lydia said Barnabas was being unreasonable, but she kept making and selling pottery from the front throwing room. She did, however, stop paying any rent until Barnabas "came to his senses." Barnabas sued Lydia for the unpaid rent. The jurisdiction followed the majority rule.', 'Should the court rule in Barnabas''s favor?',
