) VALUES (
    '22018_lakeside_lodge_risk', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Contracts for Land', 'Land-Sale Contracts',
    'Stephen owned a lakeside lodge where he had lived for many years. Deciding to move to a coastal town to be near family, he offered to sell the lodge to Lydia, a traveler who had admired it during a long stay nearby, for $190,000. After inspecting the lodge, Lydia accepted. They signed a written contract of sale setting closing of title for two months after signing. Because Stephen had already arranged to leave and Lydia had nowhere else to stay, the contract included a clause permitting Lydia to move into the lodge immediately. Lydia moved in a few days after signing. Stephen kept the fire insurance policy on the lodge in force, intending to cancel it once he conveyed title; Lydia, for her part, bought her own fire insurance policy on the lodge soon after signing. Two weeks after Lydia moved in, a fire — sparked by a lightning strike during a night storm and attributable to neither party — partially destroyed the roof, the entire kitchen, and part of the exterior porch. Lydia immediately told Stephen that she was unwilling to complete the purchase at the agreed price, but that she would renegotiate a lower price reflecting the lodge''s diminished value.', 'Stephen owned a lakeside lodge where he had lived for many years. Deciding to move to a coastal town to be near family, he offered to sell the lodge to Lydia, a traveler who had admired it during a long stay nearby, for $190,000. After inspecting the lodge, Lydia accepted. They signed a written contract of sale setting closing of title for two months after signing. Because Stephen had already arranged to leave and Lydia had nowhere else to stay, the contract included a clause permitting Lydia to move into the lodge immediately. Lydia moved in a few days after signing. Stephen kept the fire insurance policy on the lodge in force, intending to cancel it once he conveyed title; Lydia, for her part, bought her own fire insurance policy on the lodge soon after signing. Two weeks after Lydia moved in, a fire — sparked by a lightning strike during a night storm and attributable to neither party — partially destroyed the roof, the entire kitchen, and part of the exterior porch. Lydia immediately told Stephen that she was unwilling to complete the purchase at the agreed price, but that she would renegotiate a lower price reflecting the lodge''s diminished value.', 'If Stephen sues for damages based on Lydia''s anticipatory repudiation of the contract of sale, what is Stephen''s most effective argument?',
