    '22052_stephen_pottery', 'REAL_PROPERTY', 'Nature and Ownership of Land', 'Rights and duties of co-tenants',
    'Stephen was a potter who threw and fired earthenware for sale, and he occupied a workshop building that he rented for that purpose from the landlord. When the landlord told Stephen that he was thinking of selling the workshop, Stephen discussed with his brothers Andrew and Philip the possibility of buying it as an investment. After negotiation, the three brothers bought the workshop from the landlord, who conveyed it by a deed that referred to the grantees but did not specify the tenancy created. The purchase price was $144,000, of which the three brothers paid half in cash at the time the deed was delivered. At the same time, the three brothers executed and delivered a deed of trust to secure a note for the balance of the purchase price. It was the intention of the three brothers to hold the workshop until rising real estate prices made it possible for them to sell it at a profit. Until then, it was understood that Stephen would continue to occupy and use it for his pottery business. Subsequently, after requesting but not receiving contributions from his brothers, Stephen spent $7,500 to improve the workshop by adding a glazing room, and $4,000 to preserve the building by repairing the roof, which had begun to leak. In addition, Stephen alone paid principal and interest on the outstanding trust deed obligation and all real estate taxes on the property.

**Call:** If Stephen does not sue for partition but brings an appropriate action for contribution against his brothers, which of the following statements most accurately describes the rights of the parties?', 'Stephen was a potter who threw and fired earthenware for sale, and he occupied a workshop building that he rented for that purpose from the landlord. When the landlord told Stephen that he was thinking of selling the workshop, Stephen discussed with his brothers Andrew and Philip the possibility of buying it as an investment. After negotiation, the three brothers bought the workshop from the landlord, who conveyed it by a deed that referred to the grantees but did not specify the tenancy created. The purchase price was $144,000, of which the three brothers paid half in cash at the time the deed was delivered. At the same time, the three brothers executed and delivered a deed of trust to secure a note for the balance of the purchase price. It was the intention of the three brothers to hold the workshop until rising real estate prices made it possible for them to sell it at a profit. Until then, it was understood that Stephen would continue to occupy and use it for his pottery business. Subsequently, after requesting but not receiving contributions from his brothers, Stephen spent $7,500 to improve the workshop by adding a glazing room, and $4,000 to preserve the building by repairing the roof, which had begun to leak. In addition, Stephen alone paid principal and interest on the outstanding trust deed obligation and all real estate taxes on the property.

