    'Stephen owned a hillside vineyard that touched no public road; the only way his ox-carts could reach the market road was a fifteen-foot-wide cart path that Stephen had bought as an easement across Naomi''s barley field. After several harvests, Stephen — who had started floating his grapes downriver instead — told Naomi, "I won''t be needing that cart path anymore." Soon after, Stephen sold the vineyard to Philip. Philip relied on Stephen''s assertion that the cart-path easement still ran with the vineyard when he bought it. On the first day Philip owned the vineyard, he drove his ox-carts onto the path, but Naomi had built a barricade across it and told Philip she would not let the carts pass without a new agreement. Philip sued Naomi for use of the easement.', 'Stephen owned a hillside vineyard that touched no public road; the only way his ox-carts could reach the market road was a fifteen-foot-wide cart path that Stephen had bought as an easement across Naomi''s barley field. After several harvests, Stephen — who had started floating his grapes downriver instead — told Naomi, "I won''t be needing that cart path anymore." Soon after, Stephen sold the vineyard to Philip. Philip relied on Stephen''s assertion that the cart-path easement still ran with the vineyard when he bought it. On the first day Philip owned the vineyard, he drove his ox-carts onto the path, but Naomi had built a barricade across it and told Philip she would not let the carts pass without a new agreement. Philip sued Naomi for use of the easement.', 'Should the court rule in Philip''s favor?',
