) VALUES (
    '15111_gideon-repair-shed', 'TORTS', 'Other Torts', 'Contribution and indemnity',
    'Peter operates a small woodworking shop from his home. He stores his tools, including a high-powered nail gun, in an unlocked shed behind his house. Peter knows that his neighbor Daniel has recently been making threats against a local shopkeeper after a dispute over a business deal. One Saturday, Peter is loading his truck for a weekend trip to his cousin''s cabin. He leaves the nail gun on the workbench in the unlocked shed while he runs inside to grab his jacket. Daniel, walking past, sees the shed open, enters, and takes the nail gun. Later that day, Daniel uses the nail gun to seriously injure the shopkeeper. The shopkeeper recovers a judgment for his damages against both Peter and Daniel, on the ground that Peter was negligent in leaving the nail gun accessible and is therefore jointly and severally liable with Daniel for the shopkeeper''s injuries. The jurisdiction has a statute that allows contribution based upon proportionate fault and adheres to the traditional common-law rules on indemnity.

(A) Contribution only, based on comparative fault, because Peter himself was negligent.

(B) Nothing, because Peter''s negligence was a substantial proximate cause of the injury.

(C) Indemnity for the full amount of the judgment, because Daniel was an intentional tortfeasor.

(D) One-half of the amount of the judgment.', 'Peter operates a small woodworking shop from his home. He stores his tools, including a high-powered nail gun, in an unlocked shed behind his house. Peter knows that his neighbor Daniel has recently been making threats against a local shopkeeper after a dispute over a business deal. One Saturday, Peter is loading his truck for a weekend trip to his cousin''s cabin. He leaves the nail gun on the workbench in the unlocked shed while he runs inside to grab his jacket. Daniel, walking past, sees the shed open, enters, and takes the nail gun. Later that day, Daniel uses the nail gun to seriously injure the shopkeeper. The shopkeeper recovers a judgment for his damages against both Peter and Daniel, on the ground that Peter was negligent in leaving the nail gun accessible and is therefore jointly and severally liable with Daniel for the shopkeeper''s injuries. The jurisdiction has a statute that allows contribution based upon proportionate fault and adheres to the traditional common-law rules on indemnity.

(A) Contribution only, based on comparative fault, because Peter himself was negligent.

(B) Nothing, because Peter''s negligence was a substantial proximate cause of the injury.

(C) Indemnity for the full amount of the judgment, because Daniel was an intentional tortfeasor.

(D) One-half of the amount of the judgment.', 'If Peter fully satisfies the judgment, he then will have a right to recover from Daniel:',
