    'Peter, a church-van volunteer, helped Martha load a sealed lunch cooler into the back of a pickup after a women’s retreat. While Peter was closing the hatch, he negligently jostled the cooler. Unknown to anyone, the cooler held a cracked glass jar beside a small camping-stove fuel canister; the jar leaked, fumes ignited, and the cooler gave a sudden pop. Ruth, a visiting church member waiting on the far side of the parking lot beyond a row of trash cans and a low hedge, heard the pop, panicked, ran, and twisted her ankle after stepping on her own tote strap.', 'Peter, a church-van volunteer, helped Martha load a sealed lunch cooler into the back of a pickup after a women’s retreat. While Peter was closing the hatch, he negligently jostled the cooler. Unknown to anyone, the cooler held a cracked glass jar beside a small camping-stove fuel canister; the jar leaked, fumes ignited, and the cooler gave a sudden pop. Ruth, a visiting church member waiting on the far side of the parking lot beyond a row of trash cans and a low hedge, heard the pop, panicked, ran, and twisted her ankle after stepping on her own tote strap.', 'Under the common Palsgraf duty framing, is Ruth likely owed a negligence duty by Peter?',
