    '17857_living_nativity_carver', 'TORTS', 'Economic torts', 'Intentional interference with prospective advantage (competition privilege)',
    'Esther has built a hand-carved outdoor Living Nativity scene for Mary''s small church each December for the past nine years under a hand-shake annual arrangement. Each fall, Mary and Esther would agree on a price and a delivery date for the next season, but no written renewal contract was ever signed. In late summer, Paul, another local woodworker with a similar style, quoted Mary $3,200 for a comparable Living Nativity scene — $700 below Esther''s most recent price — and Mary accepted Paul''s offer. Esther now sues Paul for interference with prospective economic advantage.', 'Esther has built a hand-carved outdoor Living Nativity scene for Mary''s small church each December for the past nine years under a hand-shake annual arrangement. Each fall, Mary and Esther would agree on a price and a delivery date for the next season, but no written renewal contract was ever signed. In late summer, Paul, another local woodworker with a similar style, quoted Mary $3,200 for a comparable Living Nativity scene — $700 below Esther''s most recent price — and Mary accepted Paul''s offer. Esther now sues Paul for interference with prospective economic advantage.', 'Is Esther likely to prevail against Paul?',
