    'Joseph went to Daniel’s farm-equipment resale lot on the edge of town and bought a used 14-horsepower riding lawn tractor for $1,850 to help Hannah mow their long gravel drive and back pasture. The bill of sale Daniel gave Joseph included, in capital letters at the top, the line: "TRACTOR SOLD AS IS — NO IMPLIED WARRANTIES." When Joseph asked Daniel how the tractor ran, Daniel said, "I believe this tractor is in good working order." Hannah used the tractor to mow that Saturday, and after about two hours the engine seized. The repair bill was $720.', 'Joseph went to Daniel’s farm-equipment resale lot on the edge of town and bought a used 14-horsepower riding lawn tractor for $1,850 to help Hannah mow their long gravel drive and back pasture. The bill of sale Daniel gave Joseph included, in capital letters at the top, the line: "TRACTOR SOLD AS IS — NO IMPLIED WARRANTIES." When Joseph asked Daniel how the tractor ran, Daniel said, "I believe this tractor is in good working order." Hannah used the tractor to mow that Saturday, and after about two hours the engine seized. The repair bill was $720.', 'If Hannah asserts a claim for damages against Daniel on the grounds that Daniel breached the implied warranty of merchantability, the court should find for:',
