    '18914_rare_bible_provenance', 'TORTS', 'Economic torts', 'Fraud - reliance',
    'Esther, a retired Christian schoolteacher in New England who had been quietly selling down her late husband''s collection of antique books, orally told Daniel, a seminary student and serious Bible collector, that a 1699 Geneva Bible she was offering had "all of its original pages." Before paying, Daniel received in the mail an unsigned single-page provenance letter from a well-known third-party biblical-authentication firm; the letter was printed on firm letterhead and plainly stated on its first page: "PAGES 412-448 WERE REPLACED IN 1948; REMAINDER OF VOLUME CONSISTENT WITH 1699 ISSUE." Daniel did not read the letter. He paid Esther $4,800 for the Bible, later noticed the replaced pages, and now sues Esther for fraud.', 'Esther, a retired Christian schoolteacher in New England who had been quietly selling down her late husband''s collection of antique books, orally told Daniel, a seminary student and serious Bible collector, that a 1699 Geneva Bible she was offering had "all of its original pages." Before paying, Daniel received in the mail an unsigned single-page provenance letter from a well-known third-party biblical-authentication firm; the letter was printed on firm letterhead and plainly stated on its first page: "PAGES 412-448 WERE REPLACED IN 1948; REMAINDER OF VOLUME CONSISTENT WITH 1699 ISSUE." Daniel did not read the letter. He paid Esther $4,800 for the Bible, later noticed the replaced pages, and now sues Esther for fraud.', 'What is Esther''s strongest defense?',
