    'Galilee Trails, a private company that runs paid Christian wilderness retreats, required each participant to list an emergency contact. During a dawn prayer hike, Lydia became separated from her group. Peter, the company’s trip coordinator, mixed up two radio reports and sent Lydia’s husband, Timothy, an emergency text saying that Lydia had been found dead near the overlook. Lydia was actually alive and waiting in a ranger cabin with Ruth, a first-aid volunteer. Timothy suffered severe emotional distress from the death notice. The jurisdiction recognizes negligent erroneous death notice as a special NIED category.', 'Galilee Trails, a private company that runs paid Christian wilderness retreats, required each participant to list an emergency contact. During a dawn prayer hike, Lydia became separated from her group. Peter, the company’s trip coordinator, mixed up two radio reports and sent Lydia’s husband, Timothy, an emergency text saying that Lydia had been found dead near the overlook. Lydia was actually alive and waiting in a ranger cabin with Ruth, a first-aid volunteer. Timothy suffered severe emotional distress from the death notice. The jurisdiction recognizes negligent erroneous death notice as a special NIED category.', 'If Timothy sues Galilee Trails, how should a court likely rule on the emotional-distress claim?',
