    'Peter, a 16-year-old boy, purchased a beginner''s archery set manufactured by ArkCo. The set included a bow, six target arrows, a quiver, and a paper target. Peter invited his friend Daniel, a classmate, to his family''s rural property to practice archery. While Daniel was retrieving arrows from the target, Peter carelessly fired an arrow in Daniel''s general direction to "surprise" him. The arrow struck Daniel in the arm, causing a serious injury. The archery set was as safe as possible for its intended recreational purpose, and its benefits as an entry-level sporting product exceeded its risks. The set did not contain a warning that its arrows could cause serious bodily harm or death if fired at a person. In a suit by Daniel against ArkCo, based on strict liability, Daniel will

